package com.sushant.pmpstudy.data

object QuizBank {
    val all: List<QuizQuestion> = listOf(
        QuizQuestion("q1", "blueprint", "Your project is behind schedule. A stakeholder demands you accelerate delivery without increasing budget. What is your FIRST step?", listOf("A. Add more resources to the critical path immediately", "B. Reduce project scope to meet the deadline", "C. Communicate the implications and discuss alternatives with the stakeholder", "D. Cut corners to save time and meet the deadline"), 2, "PMI always favours communication and collaboration first. Assess the situation, communicate trade-offs and implications to the stakeholder, and collaboratively determine the best path forward. Crashing (adding resources) costs money — contradicting the \"no budget increase\" constraint. Scope reduction needs formal change control. Cutting corners violates quality standards and ethics."),
        QuizQuestion("q2", "blueprint", "A team member tells you the project budget might be exceeded next quarter if a vendor delivers late. What is this — and what do you do FIRST?", listOf("A. It is an issue — update the issue log and escalate immediately", "B. It is a risk — update the risk register and analyze it", "C. It is a constraint — update the project charter", "D. It is a problem — call an emergency team meeting now"), 1, "\"Might\" signals uncertainty about a future event — this is a RISK, not an issue. First action: document it in the Risk Register. Then perform qualitative risk analysis to prioritise it. Then plan a risk response. The Issue Log is for problems that have already occurred. Escalation is premature without analysis."),
        QuizQuestion("q3", "blueprint", "A senior executive asks you to bypass change control for a minor scope addition she personally approved. What do you do?", listOf("A. Implement the change since a senior executive approved it", "B. Submit a formal change request and follow the change control process", "C. Ask the team to implement it quietly after hours", "D. Tell the executive it is not possible under any circumstances"), 1, "ALL changes must go through Integrated Change Control regardless of who requests it or how minor it seems. The PM's job is to follow the process — not circumvent it under authority pressure. Submitting a formal change request protects the project, the PM, and ensures the impact is properly assessed and approved."),
        QuizQuestion("q4", "blueprint", "A candidate is preparing for the July 2026 PMP exam using a 2023 study guide. What is the MOST significant risk to their preparation?", listOf("A. The guide uses PMBOK 7 instead of PMBOK 8", "B. The guide under-invests in Business Environment by a factor of three", "C. The guide uses 230-minute timing instead of 240", "D. The guide covers 35 tasks instead of 26"), 1, "The most significant impact of the 2026 ECO is that Business Environment tripled from 8% to 26%. A 2023 guide would allocate minimal time to this domain, leaving the candidate unprepared for roughly a quarter of the exam. While the other changes are real, they are less impactful on actual exam performance."),
        QuizQuestion("q5", "blueprint", "During the exam, you encounter a network diagram question asking for the critical path. Activities: A(3)→B(4)→D(2) and A(3)→C(6)→D(2). What is the critical path duration?", listOf("A. 9 days", "B. 11 days", "C. 14 days", "D. 15 days"), 1, "Path 1: A→B→D = 3+4+2 = 9 days. Path 2: A→C→D = 3+6+2 = 11 days. The critical path is the LONGEST path = A→C→D = 11 days. Activities on this path (A, C, D) have zero float. Activity B has 2 days of float (11−9)."),
        QuizQuestion("q6", "blueprint", "You are 90 minutes into the exam and have answered 60 questions. You are on pace for how many questions in 240 minutes?", listOf("A. 120 questions — you will run out of time", "B. 160 questions — you will run out of time", "C. 180 questions — you are exactly on pace", "D. 200 questions — you have spare time"), 1, "60 questions in 90 min = 1.5 min per question. At that rate, 240 min ÷ 1.5 = 160 questions. You will NOT finish all 180 questions. You need to speed up to ~1.33 min/question. This is why time management practice is critical — many candidates know the content but fail on time."),
        QuizQuestion("q7", "blueprint", "On a Kanban board shown in a graphic question, the \"In Review\" column has 8 items but a WIP limit of 3. What does this indicate and what should the team do?", listOf("A. The team is highly productive — increase the WIP limit", "B. There is a bottleneck in review — the team should stop pulling new work and swarm on reviews", "C. The WIP limit was set too low — remove it", "D. The reviewers should be replaced with faster reviewers"), 1, "A column exceeding its WIP limit signals a bottleneck. In Kanban, the response is to STOP pulling new work into the system and focus the team on clearing the bottleneck (swarming). Raising the WIP limit hides the problem. Removing the limit defeats the purpose of Kanban. Replacing people is never a first response."),
        QuizQuestion("q8", "blueprint", "A case study describes a hybrid project where the infrastructure component uses predictive methods and the software component uses Scrum. A question asks how the PM should handle a change request to the infrastructure scope. What is correct?", listOf("A. Add it to the product backlog for the Product Owner to prioritise", "B. Submit a formal change request through Integrated Change Control", "C. Discuss it in the next sprint retrospective", "D. Implement it immediately since the project is agile"), 1, "In a hybrid project, the PREDICTIVE component follows predictive rules — formal change control. The infrastructure scope is predictive, so a formal change request through the CCB is correct. Only the software (Scrum) component would use backlog prioritisation. Hybrid means applying the right process to each component — not blending them randomly."),
        QuizQuestion("q9", "pmbok8", "In PMBOK 8, which performance domain absorbed both Stakeholder Management AND Communications Management from PMBOK 6?", listOf("A. Governance", "B. Resources", "C. Stakeholders", "D. Finance"), 2, "The Stakeholders performance domain in PMBOK 8 consolidates both Stakeholder Management and Communications Management from PMBOK 6. PMI recognised that communication and stakeholder engagement are inseparable — effective communication IS stakeholder engagement."),
        QuizQuestion("q10", "pmbok8", "A project team is midway through execution. A team member asks when monitoring activities should begin. What is the correct answer?", listOf("A. After execution is complete", "B. At the start of the closing focus area", "C. Monitoring runs in parallel with all focus areas throughout the entire project", "D. Monitoring begins only when problems arise"), 2, "Monitoring and Controlling is a Focus Area that runs IN PARALLEL with all other Focus Areas throughout the entire project — from initiation through closing. It is not sequential. Waiting until execution is complete or until problems arise would leave the project uncontrolled."),
        QuizQuestion("q11", "pmbok8", "Which PMBOK 8 principle explicitly addresses environmental, social, and economic impacts — considering the needs of community, society, and future generations?", listOf("A. Adopt a Holistic View", "B. Focus on Value", "C. Build an Empowered Culture", "D. Integrate Sustainability Within All Project Areas"), 3, "Principle 5 — Integrate Sustainability — specifically broadens the PM orientation to consider community, society, and posterity (future generations). It introduces the triple bottom line (people, profit, planet) and three maturity levels of PM orientation: compliance-oriented, achievement-oriented, and sustainability-oriented."),
        QuizQuestion("q12", "pmbok8", "Which PMBOK 8 performance domain is the ONLY one with processes in all five Focus Areas (Initiating through Closing)?", listOf("A. Scope", "B. Stakeholders", "C. Governance", "D. Risk"), 2, "Governance is the only domain with processes in all 5 Focus Areas: Initiate Project/Phase (Initiating), Integrate & Align Plans (Planning), Manage Execution (Executing), Monitor & Control Performance (M&C), and Close Project/Phase (Closing). It is the integrating domain — like Integration Management in PMBOK 6. Stakeholders has Initiating but not Closing. Scope, Schedule, Finance, Resources, Risk have no Initiating or Closing processes."),
        QuizQuestion("q13", "pmbok8", "A project delivers all scope on time and on budget, but the intended business benefits are never realised because market conditions changed. According to PMBOK 8, was this project successful?", listOf("A. Yes — it met all triple constraint targets", "B. No — PMBOK 8 defines success by outcomes and value, not just outputs", "C. Partially — schedule and cost were met, so it is 66% successful", "D. It depends on whether the sponsor was satisfied"), 1, "PMBOK 8 (and the Focus on Value principle) defines project success by whether it delivered VALUE and realised BENEFITS — not just whether outputs were delivered on time and on budget. A project that delivers all outputs but no value has technically failed by PMBOK 8 standards. This is a key mindset shift from PMBOK 6."),
        QuizQuestion("q14", "pmbok8", "Which PMBOK 8 term describes a negative consequence of project outputs that must be identified and managed alongside benefits?", listOf("A. Residual risk", "B. Disbenefit", "C. Negative float", "D. Adverse outcome"), 1, "A \"Disbenefit\" is a negative consequence of project outputs — a term formalised in PMBOK 8. For example, a new system may deliver efficiency benefits but create a disbenefit of staff reduction or training burden. Disbenefits must be identified in the Benefits Management Plan and managed just like benefits. Residual risk refers to remaining risk after responses; negative float relates to schedule."),
        QuizQuestion("q15", "pmbok8", "According to PMBOK 8, what is the difference between an \"outcome\" and an \"output\"?", listOf("A. They are synonyms — both mean deliverables", "B. Output is what is produced; outcome is the result/consequence/change it enables", "C. Outcome is produced by the team; output is produced by the vendor", "D. Output is tangible; outcome is always intangible"), 1, "An OUTPUT is what the project produces — the deliverable (e.g., a software application). An OUTCOME is the end result or change that the output enables (e.g., 30% reduction in processing time). PMBOK 8 emphasises that PMs must focus on outcomes and value, not just producing outputs. A perfect output that produces no desired outcome is a failed project."),
        QuizQuestion("q16", "pmbok8", "A PM is tailoring the governance approach for a small internal project in an agile organisation. According to PMBOK 8, what level of governance is appropriate?", listOf("A. Comprehensive governance — all projects need full governance", "B. Lightweight governance appropriate to adaptive methods and small scale", "C. No governance — agile projects are self-governing", "D. Moderate governance combining predictive controls with agile flexibility"), 1, "PMBOK 8 explicitly states governance should be tailored: lightweight for adaptive/agile methods and small projects; moderate for hybrid; comprehensive for large predictive portfolios. A small internal agile project needs proportionate, lightweight governance — not the full apparatus of a large regulated project. But \"no governance\" is never correct — every project needs some governance framework."),
        QuizQuestion("q17", "mindset", "Two team members have a technical conflict. You have spoken to each individually. What is your NEXT step?", listOf("A. Escalate to the project sponsor to make the final decision", "B. Force a decision by choosing the technical approach yourself", "C. Bring both team members together to collaboratively solve the issue", "D. Document the conflict in the issue log and move forward without resolution"), 2, "After meeting individually (correct first step), the next step is to bring both parties together for collaborative problem-solving — PMI's preferred conflict resolution technique. Escalation and forcing are last resorts. Documenting without resolution avoids the conflict entirely, which almost never resolves underlying issues."),
        QuizQuestion("q18", "mindset", "During a sprint retrospective, the team identifies that they consistently overcommit to sprint goals. What should the Scrum Master do?", listOf("A. Tell the team they need to work faster to meet commitments", "B. Facilitate a discussion on estimation accuracy and sustainable pace", "C. Ask the Product Owner to reduce the number of backlog items", "D. Report the performance issue to the project sponsor"), 1, "The Scrum Master facilitates retrospectives and coaches the team — this is a coaching/process improvement situation. Overcommitment is an estimation and sustainable pace issue. Facilitating a team discussion on improving their estimation approach (story points, planning poker) and calibrating velocity accurately addresses the root cause. Telling the team to work faster violates sustainable pace principles."),
        QuizQuestion("q19", "mindset", "A project manager discovers that a team member has been reporting task status as \"complete\" when work is only partially done. What should the PM do FIRST?", listOf("A. Escalate immediately to the team member's functional manager", "B. Meet with the team member privately to understand the situation and address it", "C. Remove the team member from the project for dishonesty", "D. Send an email to all stakeholders warning them that status reports may be inaccurate"), 1, "A.R.T.: First, meet privately with the team member to understand why this is happening (Assess). There may be pressure to appear on track, misunderstanding of \"done,\" or other root causes. Jumping to escalation, removal, or stakeholder announcements before understanding the situation is premature and could damage trust and morale unnecessarily."),
        QuizQuestion("q20", "mindset", "A team member from a different cultural background rarely speaks up in meetings, and other members interpret this as disengagement. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Tell the team member they need to participate more actively", "B. Recognise this may be a cultural communication style; create alternative channels for input", "C. Reassign the team member to a role with less meeting involvement", "D. Ask the team to be more direct in asking the team member for opinions"), 1, "This is a cultural intelligence scenario. In many cultures, speaking up in group meetings — especially to disagree — is inappropriate. The PM should recognise this, avoid misinterpreting silence as disengagement, and create alternative channels (one-on-one conversations, written input, smaller groups) where the team member can contribute comfortably. Telling them to change or reassigning them is culturally insensitive and violates the Respect value."),
        QuizQuestion("q21", "mindset", "During a status meeting, the sponsor angrily demands to know why the project is behind schedule and interrupts the PM repeatedly. What demonstrates emotional intelligence?", listOf("A. Match the sponsor's energy to show you take the situation seriously", "B. Stay calm, acknowledge the concern, present the facts, and propose next steps", "C. End the meeting and reschedule when the sponsor is calmer", "D. Defer to the sponsor and agree to whatever timeline they demand"), 1, "Self-regulation (staying calm) + empathy (acknowledging the concern) + social skills (presenting facts and proposing solutions) = emotional intelligence in action. Matching anger escalates conflict. Ending the meeting avoids the issue. Blindly agreeing abandons professional judgment. The EI response is calm, factual, and solution-oriented."),
        QuizQuestion("q22", "mindset", "A vendor you are evaluating for a large contract sends you an expensive gift. Your organisation has no explicit gift policy. What should you do?", listOf("A. Accept the gift — there is no policy prohibiting it", "B. Accept the gift but do not let it influence your evaluation", "C. Decline the gift and document the offer; consult with your ethics officer or manager", "D. Accept the gift and share it with the team"), 2, "Fairness and Honesty values of the PMI Code of Ethics apply. Even without an explicit policy, accepting an expensive gift from a vendor under evaluation creates an actual or perceived conflict of interest. The correct action: decline, document the offer, and consult with appropriate authority. \"No policy\" does not mean \"no ethical obligation.\" The exam always rewards the option that avoids even the appearance of impropriety."),
        QuizQuestion("q23", "mindset", "A PM discovers their team has been reporting task status as \"on track\" to hide delays, hoping to catch up. The sponsor is unaware. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Allow the team to catch up before informing the sponsor — no harm done", "B. Immediately report the true status to the sponsor and address the reporting culture with the team", "C. Reprimand the team members publicly for dishonesty", "D. Continue the current reports and quietly work to close the gap"), 1, "Honesty is non-negotiable. The PM must report accurate status to the sponsor immediately — hiding delays violates the Honesty value and prevents the sponsor from making informed decisions. Separately, the PM must address the team culture that led to misreporting — understanding WHY they felt pressure to hide delays (psychological safety issue?). Public reprimand violates Respect and damages trust further."),
        QuizQuestion("q24", "mindset", "Which of the following BEST demonstrates the PMI value of Responsibility?", listOf("A. Reporting a project as green when it is yellow to protect the team from criticism", "B. Declining to make a decision until the sponsor provides direction", "C. Taking ownership of a scheduling error you made and proposing a recovery plan", "D. Delegating a difficult stakeholder conversation to a team member"), 2, "Responsibility means taking ownership of decisions and their consequences. Acknowledging your own error and proposing a solution demonstrates this. Misreporting (A) violates Honesty. Deferring all decisions (B) abdicates responsibility. Delegating difficult conversations (D) avoids accountability. The Responsibility value requires PMs to own outcomes — good and bad."),
        QuizQuestion("q25", "integration", "A sponsor verbally approves a significant scope change and tells the PM to proceed immediately. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Proceed — the sponsor has authority to approve changes", "B. Document the approval in meeting minutes and proceed", "C. Submit a formal change request to the CCB for review and approval", "D. Ask the team to start while submitting the change request in parallel"), 2, "ALL scope changes require a formal change request through Integrated Change Control — including changes verbally approved by the sponsor. The CCB must formally review and approve. The PM's job is to protect the project and follow the process — even under sponsor pressure. Starting work without approval risks wasted effort if the change is rejected."),
        QuizQuestion("q26", "integration", "What is the sequence of: Work Performance Data, Work Performance Information, Work Performance Reports?", listOf("A. Reports → Data → Information", "B. Information → Data → Reports", "C. Data → Information → Reports", "D. Reports → Information → Data"), 2, "The correct sequence is: Work Performance DATA (raw observations collected during executing) → analyzed into Work Performance INFORMATION (EVM metrics, variances — produced in M&C) → formatted into Work Performance REPORTS (communicated to stakeholders). This flow shows how raw data becomes actionable communication."),
        QuizQuestion("q27", "integration", "At project closure, what must happen BEFORE transitioning deliverables to the operations team?", listOf("A. All project resources must be released", "B. Formal acceptance of deliverables from the customer or sponsor", "C. The project budget must be fully spent or returned", "D. Lessons learned must be documented first"), 1, "Formal acceptance of deliverables is the FIRST step in project closure — you cannot transition something to operations that hasn't been formally accepted. Lessons learned, resource release, and contract closure all follow acceptance. This is the order: Acceptance → Lessons Learned → Transition → Disband team."),
        QuizQuestion("q28", "integration", "A quality inspection finds a defect in a delivered component. The PM wants to fix it. What type of change request is this?", listOf("A. Corrective action", "B. Preventive action", "C. Defect repair", "D. Update"), 2, "Fixing a nonconforming product component is a DEFECT REPAIR. Corrective action realigns project PERFORMANCE (schedule, cost) with the plan. Preventive action reduces probability of FUTURE problems. Update modifies documents. All four go through Integrated Change Control — defect repair is not exempt."),
        QuizQuestion("q29", "integration", "The PM identifies that a risky phase is approaching and wants to add extra testing before it begins to prevent problems. What type of change request is this?", listOf("A. Corrective action", "B. Preventive action", "C. Defect repair", "D. Scope change"), 1, "Adding a measure to REDUCE THE PROBABILITY of future problems is PREVENTIVE ACTION. It is proactive — no problem exists yet. Corrective action would apply if a problem already existed and needed correction. Preventive action still requires CCB approval because it modifies the plan (adds work/time/cost)."),
        QuizQuestion("q30", "integration", "A stakeholder asks the PM: \"Who has the authority to approve a change to the cost baseline?\" Where should the PM look for the answer?", listOf("A. The Project Charter", "B. The Cost Management Plan", "C. The Change Management Plan", "D. The Stakeholder Register"), 2, "The Change Management Plan (a component of the Project Management Plan) defines the change control process, including who has authority to approve which types of changes, CCB composition, and escalation thresholds. The Project Charter gives the PM general authority but not the specific change approval process. The Cost Management Plan defines how costs are managed, not who approves changes."),
        QuizQuestion("q31", "integration", "A project is at a phase gate. The steering committee reviews performance and finds the business case is no longer valid due to market changes. What is the appropriate decision?", listOf("A. Continue — sunk costs must be recovered", "B. Terminate the project — the business case no longer justifies investment", "C. Reduce scope and continue with a smaller project", "D. Pause the project until market conditions improve"), 1, "Phase gates exist precisely to make continue/modify/terminate decisions. If the business case is no longer valid, terminating is the correct choice — continuing wastes resources on a project that cannot deliver value. Sunk costs are irrelevant to forward-looking decisions (sunk cost fallacy). Reducing scope or pausing might be considered, but the question specifies the business case is INVALID — not diminished."),
        QuizQuestion("q32", "integration", "Which statement about the PMIS is correct?", listOf("A. The PMIS is an output of the Develop Project Management Plan process", "B. The PMIS is an Enterprise Environmental Factor and a tool used across many processes", "C. The PMIS must be built from scratch for each project", "D. The PMIS is only used in predictive projects"), 1, "The PMIS is part of the organisational infrastructure — an Enterprise Environmental Factor (EEF). It is used as a tool/technique across many processes (scheduling, tracking, communication). It is not an output of any process. It exists at the organisational level and supports all project types — predictive, adaptive, and hybrid."),
        QuizQuestion("q33", "scope", "A developer adds a visually appealing animation to a web app that was not in the approved requirements. The customer did not request it. This is:", listOf("A. Scope creep — the customer is adding unauthorised features", "B. Gold plating — the team is adding extras beyond agreed scope", "C. Progressive elaboration — normal planning refinement", "D. Requirements creep — stakeholder-driven scope expansion"), 1, "Gold plating is adding features beyond what was agreed in the scope baseline — even with good intentions. The developer decided (without authorisation) to add something extra. Gold plating wastes resources, introduces unreviewed risk, and may not align with customer priorities or technical constraints. Scope creep involves the customer or stakeholders requesting unauthorised additions."),
        QuizQuestion("q34", "scope", "Control Quality has been completed and Verified Deliverables produced. What is the NEXT step?", listOf("A. Release the deliverables to operations", "B. Perform Validate Scope to get formal customer acceptance", "C. Update the lessons learned register", "D. Archive the project documents"), 1, "After Control Quality produces Verified Deliverables, the next step is Validate Scope — where the customer or sponsor formally accepts the deliverables. Only Accepted Deliverables (the output of Validate Scope) can be transitioned to operations or used to close the project. You cannot close the project without formal acceptance."),
        QuizQuestion("q35", "scope", "Which document provides the detailed description of each WBS work package — including owner, estimated cost, duration, and acceptance criteria?", listOf("A. Project Scope Statement", "B. Work Breakdown Structure", "C. WBS Dictionary", "D. Requirements Traceability Matrix"), 2, "The WBS Dictionary provides detailed descriptions of each component in the WBS, including: description, deliverables, accepting organisation, schedule milestones, associated schedule activities, resources required, cost estimates, quality requirements, acceptance criteria, technical references, and agreement information. Together with the Project Scope Statement and WBS, it forms the Scope Baseline."),
        QuizQuestion("q36", "scope", "A PM wants to gather requirements from 200 end users across 5 countries. Which elicitation technique is MOST appropriate?", listOf("A. Individual interviews with each user", "B. Facilitated workshop with all users", "C. Questionnaires / surveys", "D. Observation of each user"), 2, "For a large, geographically dispersed audience, questionnaires/surveys are most efficient. Interviews with 200 people would take months. A single workshop with 200 people across 5 countries is impractical. Observation of 200 users is not feasible. Surveys can reach all users efficiently and generate quantitative data."),
        QuizQuestion("q37", "scope", "Requirements gathering has stalled because a senior stakeholder dominates every meeting and others are afraid to disagree. Which technique should the PM use?", listOf("A. Facilitated workshop with the senior stakeholder as facilitator", "B. Delphi technique — anonymous, iterative expert input", "C. Brainstorming with the senior stakeholder excluded", "D. Interviews with only the senior stakeholder"), 1, "The Delphi technique uses anonymous, iterative rounds of input — it specifically eliminates the influence of dominant personalities and groupthink. Participants provide opinions anonymously, a facilitator summarises, and rounds repeat until consensus. This is the textbook solution when a dominant stakeholder is suppressing input from others."),
        QuizQuestion("q38", "scope", "Using MoSCoW prioritisation, a product owner classifies a feature as \"Could have.\" What does this mean?", listOf("A. The feature is mandatory for the release", "B. The feature is important but the release can succeed without it", "C. The feature is desirable if time and resources permit, but not important", "D. The feature will not be included in this release"), 2, "MoSCoW: MUST have = mandatory. SHOULD have = important but not critical. COULD have = desirable if time permits, minimal impact if excluded. WON'T have = explicitly excluded this time. \"Could have\" features are the first to be dropped if the sprint is running out of capacity."),
        QuizQuestion("q39", "scope", "A backlog item is pulled into a sprint but the team discovers midway that the acceptance criteria are unclear and a key dependency is unresolved. What should have prevented this?", listOf("A. A stronger Definition of Done", "B. A Definition of Ready", "C. A more detailed Sprint Goal", "D. Better Sprint Planning timeboxing"), 1, "The Definition of Ready (DoR) defines what a backlog item must have BEFORE being pulled into a sprint — clear acceptance criteria, estimates, and resolved dependencies are typical DoR criteria. If the team had applied a DoR, this item would not have been accepted into the sprint until it was ready. DoD applies at the END (completion criteria), not the start."),
        QuizQuestion("q40", "scope", "Which technique would BEST help a product team identify features that would DELIGHT customers versus features customers merely expect?", listOf("A. MoSCoW", "B. Kano Model", "C. WSJF", "D. Paired Comparison"), 1, "The Kano Model specifically categorises features into: Basic (expected — absence causes dissatisfaction, presence is neutral), Performance (more is better — linear satisfaction), and Excitement/Delighters (unexpected — presence creates delight, absence is neutral). It is the only technique designed to distinguish \"expected\" from \"delighting\" features."),
        QuizQuestion("q41", "schedule", "Activity A has total float of 0 days. Activity B has total float of 5 days. Which statement is correct?", listOf("A. Both activities are on the critical path", "B. Only Activity B is on the critical path", "C. Only Activity A is on the critical path", "D. Neither activity is on the critical path"), 2, "Critical path activities have ZERO total float — any delay to them delays the project end date. Activity A (0 float) is on the critical path. Activity B (5 days float) can be delayed up to 5 days without impacting the project end date — it is NOT on the critical path. The PM should focus attention on Activity A."),
        QuizQuestion("q42", "schedule", "A project must be shortened by 2 weeks. The sponsor says no additional budget is available. Which technique should the PM use?", listOf("A. Crashing — add resources to critical path activities", "B. Resource leveling — redistribute existing resources", "C. Fast Tracking — perform some sequential activities in parallel", "D. Parametric estimating — recalculate durations"), 2, "Fast Tracking performs activities in parallel that were planned sequentially. It does NOT add direct cost — making it the right choice when budget cannot be increased. The risk is increased rework if earlier work needs to change while later work is already underway. Crashing would require additional budget. Resource leveling typically EXTENDS the schedule."),
        QuizQuestion("q43", "schedule", "Using PERT estimation with O=10, M=15, P=20, what is the estimated duration and standard deviation?", listOf("A. Duration = 15 days, σ = 1.67 days", "B. Duration = 15 days, σ = 3.33 days", "C. Duration = 14.5 days, σ = 1.67 days", "D. Duration = 15 days, σ = 10 days"), 0, "PERT = (O + 4M + P)/6 = (10 + 60 + 20)/6 = 90/6 = 15 days. Standard Deviation σ = (P − O)/6 = (20 − 10)/6 = 10/6 = 1.67 days. When O, M, and P are symmetric around the most likely value, PERT equals the most likely estimate (15 days in this case)."),
        QuizQuestion("q44", "schedule", "Activity X has ES=5, EF=10, LS=8, LF=13. What is the total float and is it on the critical path?", listOf("A. Float = 3, not critical", "B. Float = 5, not critical", "C. Float = 0, critical", "D. Float = 8, not critical"), 0, "Total Float = LS − ES = 8 − 5 = 3 (or LF − EF = 13 − 10 = 3). Activity X can be delayed up to 3 days without delaying the project. Since float > 0, it is NOT on the critical path. Critical path activities have zero float."),
        QuizQuestion("q45", "schedule", "A team has a remaining backlog of 150 story points. Velocity over the last 4 sprints: 25, 30, 28, 27. Sprints are 2 weeks. How many weeks to complete?", listOf("A. 10 weeks", "B. 11 weeks", "C. 12 weeks", "D. 15 weeks"), 2, "Average velocity = (25+30+28+27)/4 = 110/4 = 27.5 points per sprint. Sprints remaining = 150 ÷ 27.5 = 5.45 sprints. You cannot run a partial sprint, so round UP to 6 sprints. 6 sprints × 2 weeks = 12 weeks. Always use average velocity (not best or worst) and always round up."),
        QuizQuestion("q46", "schedule", "A construction project must pour the foundation before erecting the steel frame. This dependency is:", listOf("A. Discretionary — the team could choose a different sequence", "B. Mandatory — physically required by the nature of the work", "C. External — depends on the concrete supplier", "D. Internal — within the team's control"), 1, "The foundation must physically exist before the frame can be erected — this is a MANDATORY (hard logic) dependency inherent in the nature of the work. It cannot be changed by preference. Discretionary dependencies are preferred sequences that could be altered. External dependencies involve parties outside the project. The concrete supplier relationship is separate from this physical dependency."),
        QuizQuestion("q47", "schedule", "A PM needs to compress the schedule and identifies that Design (mandatory dependency to Development) and Documentation (discretionary dependency to Development) both precede Development. Which can be fast-tracked?", listOf("A. Design — since it comes first", "B. Documentation — since it is a discretionary dependency", "C. Both can be fast-tracked equally", "D. Neither — fast-tracking always increases risk"), 1, "Fast-tracking overlaps activities. Only DISCRETIONARY dependencies can be safely overlapped because they are preferences, not requirements. Documentation → Development is discretionary, so Development can start while Documentation continues. Design → Development is mandatory — Development cannot start without completed Design. Fast-tracking mandatory dependencies causes rework or failure."),
        QuizQuestion("q48", "schedule", "A release burnup chart shows the \"scope\" line jumped up by 40 points in Sprint 5 while the \"completed\" line continued at a steady slope. What does this indicate?", listOf("A. The team's velocity increased in Sprint 5", "B. Scope was added to the release in Sprint 5", "C. The team removed completed items from the chart", "D. The chart has a data error"), 1, "In a burnup chart, the scope line represents total work in the release. When it jumps upward, scope was ADDED (new stories accepted into the release). The completed line continuing at its steady slope means velocity was unchanged. This is exactly what burnup charts are designed to reveal — scope changes that burndown charts hide."),
        QuizQuestion("q49", "cost", "EV = \$60,000 | AC = \$80,000 | PV = \$70,000 | BAC = \$150,000. What is the CPI and what does it mean?", listOf("A. CPI = 0.75 — project is over budget", "B. CPI = 0.86 — project is behind schedule", "C. CPI = 1.17 — project is under budget", "D. CPI = 1.33 — project is ahead of schedule"), 0, "CPI = EV / AC = 60,000 / 80,000 = 0.75. A CPI less than 1.0 means OVER BUDGET — for every \$1 spent, only \$0.75 of value is produced. This is a cost efficiency issue. The SPI = EV/PV = 60,000/70,000 = 0.857, meaning also behind schedule. Both metrics are negative."),
        QuizQuestion("q50", "cost", "Using the same figures (EV=\$60K, AC=\$80K, BAC=\$150K, CPI=0.75), what is the Estimate at Completion (assuming current CPI continues)?", listOf("A. \$150,000", "B. \$170,000", "C. \$200,000", "D. \$187,500"), 2, "EAC (typical) = BAC / CPI = 150,000 / 0.75 = \$200,000. This assumes the project continues at the current cost efficiency (CPI = 0.75) for the remainder. The project is forecast to cost \$50,000 more than planned (VAC = 150,000 − 200,000 = −\$50,000)."),
        QuizQuestion("q51", "cost", "A project has BAC=\$500K, EV=\$200K, AC=\$180K. The TCPI needed to meet the BAC is calculated as:", listOf("A. 0.94 — easier than current performance", "B. 1.00 — must maintain current performance", "C. 0.97 — slightly easier than current", "D. 1.06 — must improve on current performance"), 0, "TCPI (to BAC) = (BAC − EV) / (BAC − AC) = (500,000 − 200,000) / (500,000 − 180,000) = 300,000 / 320,000 = 0.9375. A TCPI less than 1.0 means the remaining work needs to be done at LESS efficiency than currently — the project can afford to be slightly less efficient and still meet the BAC. This is a relatively good position (CPI = 200,000/180,000 = 1.11 — already under budget)."),
        QuizQuestion("q52", "cost", "A project has BAC=\$400K, EV=\$160K, AC=\$200K. The PM notes that cost overruns to date were caused by a one-time equipment failure that has been resolved and will not recur. What is the EAC?", listOf("A. \$500,000", "B. \$440,000", "C. \$400,000", "D. \$360,000"), 1, "The variance is ATYPICAL (one-time, will not recur), so use: EAC = AC + (BAC − EV) = 200,000 + (400,000 − 160,000) = 200,000 + 240,000 = \$440,000. Remaining work will be done at the PLANNED rate, not the current (poor) CPI. If you used BAC/CPI = 400,000/0.8 = \$500,000 — that assumes the overrun continues, which the scenario explicitly says it won't."),
        QuizQuestion("q53", "cost", "Two projects are being compared. Project A: NPV = \$500K, payback 3 years. Project B: NPV = \$300K, payback 1.5 years. Using PMI's preferred selection criterion, which should be chosen?", listOf("A. Project B — faster payback reduces risk", "B. Project A — higher NPV creates more value", "C. Both are equal — need more information", "D. Project B — lower NPV means lower risk"), 1, "PMI prefers NPV as the primary project selection criterion because it accounts for time value of money and total value created. Project A has \$200K higher NPV. Payback period is a secondary consideration — it ignores time value of money and cash flows after payback. Unless the question specifies a cash-flow constraint, NPV wins."),
        QuizQuestion("q54", "cost", "A PM proposes continuing a struggling project because \"we have already invested \$2M and cannot let that go to waste.\" What decision-making error is this?", listOf("A. Opportunity cost fallacy", "B. Sunk cost fallacy", "C. Confirmation bias", "D. Escalation of commitment"), 1, "The Sunk Cost Fallacy — allowing already-spent, unrecoverable money to influence forward-looking decisions. The \$2M is gone regardless of whether the project continues. The only relevant question is: will FUTURE investment generate sufficient FUTURE value? PMI expects PMs to make decisions based on expected future value, not past spending. (Escalation of commitment is closely related but the specific reasoning here is sunk cost.)"),
        QuizQuestion("q55", "cost", "Which component is NOT included in the cost baseline?", listOf("A. Activity cost estimates", "B. Contingency reserve", "C. Management reserve", "D. Work package costs"), 2, "The Cost Baseline = activity estimates + work package costs + contingency reserves. Management Reserve is added ON TOP of the cost baseline to form the Project Budget — it is NOT part of the baseline. EVM measures against the baseline, so management reserve is excluded from EV calculations. This is one of the most common exam traps in Cost Management."),
        QuizQuestion("q56", "cost", "A project has a BCR of 0.85. What does this mean for the go/no-go decision?", listOf("A. Approve — BCR shows benefits exceed costs", "B. Reject — benefits are less than costs", "C. Approve — 85% ROI is excellent", "D. Need to calculate NPV before deciding"), 1, "BCR = PV of Benefits ÷ PV of Costs. A BCR of 0.85 means benefits are only 85% of costs — for every \$1 invested, only \$0.85 of benefit is returned. BCR 1 is required for a financially sound project. BCR is not the same as ROI."),
        QuizQuestion("q57", "quality", "During quality audits, it is determined that the team is not following agreed-upon processes. Which quality process does a quality audit belong to?", listOf("A. Plan Quality Management", "B. Control Quality", "C. Manage Quality", "D. Validate Scope"), 2, "Quality audits are a key tool of Manage Quality (Executing process). Audits examine whether quality PROCESSES are being followed correctly — they look at HOW the work is being done. Control Quality inspects the actual DELIVERABLES/OUTPUTS. Manage Quality focuses on the quality of processes and procedures."),
        QuizQuestion("q58", "quality", "Seven consecutive data points appear on the same side of the control chart mean, but all are within control limits. What should the PM conclude?", listOf("A. The process is in control — no action required", "B. The process is out of control — investigate immediately", "C. The control limits need to be widened", "D. The specification limits have been exceeded"), 1, "The Rule of Seven states that seven or more consecutive data points on the same side of the mean indicate the process is OUT OF CONTROL — even if all points are within the control limits. This non-random pattern signals an assignable (identifiable) cause that must be investigated and corrected. A truly random process would not produce 7 consecutive points on one side."),
        QuizQuestion("q59", "quality", "A customer complaint is received after delivery, requiring a product recall and warranty replacement. Which cost of quality category is this?", listOf("A. Prevention cost", "B. Appraisal cost", "C. Internal failure cost", "D. External failure cost"), 3, "External Failure Costs occur AFTER the product has been delivered to the customer — warranty claims, recalls, customer complaints, liability. These are the most expensive type of quality failure. Internal failure costs (rework, scrap) occur before delivery. Prevention and appraisal costs are conformance costs that try to avoid failures."),
        QuizQuestion("q60", "quality", "A budget smartphone with basic features works perfectly and reliably. A premium smartphone with advanced features crashes frequently. Which statement is correct?", listOf("A. The budget phone is low quality; the premium phone is high quality", "B. The budget phone is low grade but high quality; the premium phone is high grade but low quality", "C. Both phones are the same quality — grade is what differs", "D. Quality and grade are the same concept"), 1, "GRADE = category based on features (budget = low grade, premium = high grade). QUALITY = degree to which the product meets requirements and works as intended. The budget phone WORKS (high quality, low grade). The premium phone CRASHES (low quality, high grade). Low grade is acceptable — it is a design choice. Low quality is NEVER acceptable — it is a defect."),
        QuizQuestion("q61", "quality", "A measuring instrument consistently reads 2mm higher than the true value across all measurements. The instrument is:", listOf("A. Accurate but not precise", "B. Precise but not accurate", "C. Both accurate and precise", "D. Neither accurate nor precise"), 1, "PRECISE = consistent, repeatable measurements (tight cluster). ACCURATE = close to the true value. The instrument gives CONSISTENT readings (precise) but they are all WRONG by the same amount (not accurate). This is systematic bias — precision without accuracy. A calibration would fix accuracy while maintaining precision."),
        QuizQuestion("q62", "quality", "Which quality guru is associated with the concept \"Quality is Free\" and Zero Defects?", listOf("A. W. Edwards Deming", "B. Joseph Juran", "C. Philip Crosby", "D. Kaoru Ishikawa"), 2, "Philip Crosby wrote \"Quality is Free\" and championed Zero Defects. His argument: the cost of preventing defects is always less than the cost of fixing them — so quality effectively pays for itself. Deming is known for PDCA and 14 Points. Juran for the Quality Trilogy and \"fitness for use.\" Ishikawa for the fishbone diagram and quality circles."),
        QuizQuestion("q63", "quality", "A software team consistently takes shortcuts to meet sprint deadlines, accumulating code that \"works but is messy.\" What should the Scrum Master ensure happens?", listOf("A. Ignore it — working software is what matters", "B. Track technical debt in the backlog and allocate capacity to address it", "C. Stop all feature work until all technical debt is repaid", "D. Report the team to management for poor quality"), 1, "Technical debt (shortcuts that must be repaid later) is a real agile concept. It should be TRACKED visibly (in the backlog) and ADDRESSED through allocated capacity in sprints — not ignored, and not by stopping all feature delivery. The Scrum Master coaches the team and PO on balancing new features with technical debt reduction. Reporting to management violates servant leadership."),
        QuizQuestion("q64", "quality", "During Control Quality, an inspector uses a sample of 50 units out of 5,000 to determine if the batch meets specifications. This is an example of:", listOf("A. Attribute sampling", "B. Variable sampling", "C. Statistical sampling", "D. Design of Experiments"), 2, "Statistical sampling = inspecting a representative subset (50 of 5,000) rather than 100% of units, to draw conclusions about the whole population. It reduces the cost of quality (appraisal cost) while providing adequate confidence. Attribute vs Variable sampling describes HOW each unit is measured (pass/fail vs continuous scale). DOE is for identifying influential variables in design."),
        QuizQuestion("q65", "risk", "A project team decides to purchase insurance to cover potential damage to equipment on site. Which risk response strategy is this?", listOf("A. Avoid — eliminating the threat entirely", "B. Mitigate — reducing the probability of damage", "C. Transfer — shifting the financial impact to a third party", "D. Accept — acknowledging the risk without action"), 2, "Purchasing insurance is a Transfer strategy — the financial consequence of the risk (equipment damage) is shifted to the insurance company (third party). Transfer does NOT eliminate the risk — it shifts the monetary impact. The risk of physical damage still exists; only the financial burden is transferred."),
        QuizQuestion("q66", "risk", "After implementing a risk mitigation strategy, the team identifies a new risk that emerged as a direct result of the mitigation actions. This new risk is called a:", listOf("A. Residual risk", "B. Secondary risk", "C. Workaround risk", "D. Contingency risk"), 1, "A Secondary Risk is a new risk that arises as a DIRECT RESULT of implementing a risk response. Both secondary and residual risks must be identified, analyzed, and responded to. Residual risk is what remains after the response — not something NEW created by it. A workaround is an unplanned response to an unidentified risk that occurs."),
        QuizQuestion("q67", "risk", "A project has two risk response options. Option A: 70% probability of \$200,000 savings. Option B: 90% probability of \$100,000 savings. Using EMV, which option should the PM recommend?", listOf("A. Option A — EMV = \$140,000 (higher expected value)", "B. Option B — EMV = \$90,000 (higher probability of success)", "C. Option A — because it has higher potential savings", "D. Option B — because it is more certain to succeed"), 0, "EMV (Option A) = 70% × \$200,000 = \$140,000. EMV (Option B) = 90% × \$100,000 = \$90,000. Option A has a higher Expected Monetary Value (\$140K vs \$90K), making it the better choice from a risk analysis perspective. PMI expects you to use quantitative analysis (EMV) to make objective decisions, not just choose the \"safer\" or \"higher potential\" option."),
        QuizQuestion("q68", "risk", "A team uses a checklist based on lessons learned from five previous similar projects to identify risks. What is the primary LIMITATION of this technique?", listOf("A. It is too time-consuming", "B. It only identifies risks that occurred in past projects — new or unique risks may be missed", "C. It requires expert facilitation", "D. It cannot identify positive risks"), 1, "Checklists are efficient but limited to historically known risks. They may miss NEW risks unique to this project's context, technology, or environment. PMI recommends using checklists as a STARTING point supplemented by brainstorming, interviews, and other techniques. Checklists can include positive risks. They are quick, not time-consuming."),
        QuizQuestion("q69", "risk", "A risk has probability 2/5 and impact 5/5 (catastrophic). Another risk has probability 5/5 and impact 2/5. Both have the same P×I score. How should the PM treat them?", listOf("A. Identically — same score means same priority", "B. Prioritise the catastrophic-impact risk for a contingency plan despite lower probability", "C. Prioritise the high-probability risk since it is more likely to occur", "D. Ignore both — scores of 10 are medium priority"), 1, "While P×I scores are equal, a CATASTROPHIC impact (even at lower probability) typically warrants more attention — the consequence of occurrence could be project-ending. PMI expects PMs to apply judgment beyond raw scores. The high-probability low-impact risk can often be accepted or handled with simple mitigation. The catastrophic risk needs a contingency plan."),
        QuizQuestion("q70", "risk", "A team is uncertain whether a new database technology will perform adequately. Rather than committing the whole sprint, they allocate 2 days to build a proof of concept. This is called a:", listOf("A. Prototype", "B. Spike", "C. Sprint 0", "D. Technical debt"), 1, "A SPIKE is a time-boxed investigation or research activity in agile — used to reduce uncertainty (technical or functional) before committing to a full implementation. It is a risk reduction technique specific to agile. The output is knowledge, not a shippable increment. A prototype is a working model; Sprint 0 is a preparatory sprint; technical debt is accumulated shortcuts."),
        QuizQuestion("q71", "risk", "Monte Carlo simulation shows a 50% probability of finishing by March 15 and 90% probability by April 20. The sponsor wants a committed date. What should the PM communicate?", listOf("A. Commit to March 15 — it is the most likely date", "B. Commit to April 20 — 90% confidence is appropriate for a commitment", "C. Commit to the midpoint — April 2", "D. Refuse to commit — the simulation shows uncertainty"), 1, "For a COMMITTED date, a high confidence level (typically 80–90%) is appropriate. March 15 has only 50% confidence — a coin flip. April 20 has 90% confidence — a reliable commitment. PMI expects PMs to communicate commitments at appropriate confidence levels. Refusing to commit is not helpful; the simulation exists precisely to enable informed commitments."),
        QuizQuestion("q72", "risk", "Which risk identification technique specifically helps identify BOTH internal strengths/weaknesses AND external opportunities/threats?", listOf("A. Root cause analysis", "B. SWOT analysis", "C. Assumption analysis", "D. Prompt lists"), 1, "SWOT (Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats) analysis examines both INTERNAL factors (Strengths and Weaknesses of the organisation/project) and EXTERNAL factors (Opportunities and Threats from the environment). It is one of the few techniques explicitly designed to surface both positive risks (opportunities) and negative risks (threats)."),
        QuizQuestion("q73", "stakeholders", "A project has 10 stakeholders. Three new stakeholders are identified. How many total communication channels now exist?", listOf("A. 45 channels", "B. 78 channels", "C. 66 channels", "D. 91 channels"), 1, "New team size = 10 + 3 = 13. Channels = n(n−1)/2 = 13 × 12 / 2 = 78 channels. Original channels = 10 × 9 / 2 = 45. Adding 3 stakeholders created 33 new channels (78−45). This illustrates why large project teams have significant communication management challenges."),
        QuizQuestion("q74", "stakeholders", "A key stakeholder is classified as \"Resistant\" in the engagement assessment matrix. The desired level is \"Supportive.\" What is the BEST approach?", listOf("A. Reduce their involvement to avoid disruption to the project", "B. Escalate their resistance to the project sponsor immediately", "C. Meet with them to understand concerns and address the root cause of their resistance", "D. Send more frequent status reports to keep them better informed"), 2, "A resistant stakeholder needs targeted, personal engagement — not reduced involvement (which increases their negative influence) or escalation (premature). The PM should meet face-to-face, understand their specific concerns, involve them in relevant decisions, and work proactively to move them toward a neutral or supportive position. More email reports alone will not change a resistant mindset."),
        QuizQuestion("q75", "stakeholders", "A PM sends a weekly status report via email to 40 stakeholders. Which communication method is being used?", listOf("A. Interactive — real-time two-way exchange", "B. Pull — stakeholders access when needed", "C. Push — sender distributes to recipients without confirmation of understanding", "D. Formal — written and documented communication"), 2, "Distributing an email status report is a Push communication method — information is sent to recipients whether or not they read it or understand it. There is no confirmation of understanding. Pull communication requires recipients to actively access information themselves (e.g., SharePoint). Interactive communication involves real-time, two-way exchange. \"Formal\" is a description of style, not a PMI communication method category."),
        QuizQuestion("q76", "stakeholders", "A regulator has high authority over your project but shows little interest in day-to-day activities. According to the Power/Interest grid, what strategy should the PM use?", listOf("A. Manage closely — regulators are always high priority", "B. Keep satisfied — meet their requirements without overwhelming them", "C. Keep informed — send them all project updates", "D. Monitor — they do not need engagement"), 1, "High Power + Low Interest = \"Keep Satisfied.\" The regulator has authority (can shut down the project) but does not want daily details. The strategy: ensure their compliance requirements are met, provide required reports at required intervals, but do not flood them with information. Managing closely would waste both parties' time. Monitoring alone risks missing a compliance requirement."),
        QuizQuestion("q77", "stakeholders", "Using the Salience Model, a stakeholder has Power and Legitimacy but their claims are not Urgent. How are they classified?", listOf("A. Definitive stakeholder", "B. Dominant stakeholder", "C. Dependent stakeholder", "D. Dangerous stakeholder"), 1, "Salience Model: Power + Legitimacy (no Urgency) = DOMINANT. Power + Legitimacy + Urgency = DEFINITIVE (highest priority). Legitimacy + Urgency (no Power) = DEPENDENT. Power + Urgency (no Legitimacy) = DANGEROUS. The dominant stakeholder has authority and a legitimate claim but no pressing need — they expect to be involved but not urgently."),
        QuizQuestion("q78", "stakeholders", "A PM sends a detailed email about a critical decision. The recipient later says they never understood the email. Who bears primary responsibility for this communication failure?", listOf("A. The recipient — they should have asked for clarification", "B. The sender — they should have confirmed understanding", "C. Neither — email is an unreliable medium", "D. Both equally — communication is a shared responsibility"), 1, "In PMI's communication model, the SENDER is responsible for ensuring the message is clearly encoded AND confirming it was understood (seeking feedback/acknowledgement). For a CRITICAL decision, the PM should have used interactive communication (meeting/call) or at minimum followed up to confirm understanding. The receiver has a role, but the sender initiated and bears primary responsibility."),
        QuizQuestion("q79", "stakeholders", "According to research commonly cited in PMP prep, what percentage of communication meaning comes from words alone?", listOf("A. 7%", "B. 38%", "C. 55%", "D. 93%"), 0, "Mehrabian's research (often cited): 7% words, 38% tone of voice, 55% body language/facial expression. This is why face-to-face communication is preferred for sensitive or complex topics — 93% of meaning is lost in text-only communication. It reinforces the PMI preference for interactive, in-person communication in conflict and negotiation."),
        QuizQuestion("q80", "stakeholders", "An agile team's stakeholders complain they never know project status. The team has daily standups and sprint reviews. What should the Scrum Master recommend?", listOf("A. Invite stakeholders to the Daily Scrum", "B. Create information radiators (visible burndown charts, Kanban boards) accessible to stakeholders", "C. Send stakeholders detailed daily email reports", "D. Hold weekly stakeholder status meetings in addition to sprint reviews"), 1, "Information radiators — big visible charts (burndown, Kanban board, release burnup) placed where stakeholders can see them — are the agile answer to status transparency. They provide pull-based, real-time visibility without adding meetings. Daily Scrum is for the team, not stakeholders. Daily emails and additional meetings add overhead contrary to agile principles."),
        QuizQuestion("q81", "procurement", "A project has highly uncertain and evolving scope requirements. The PM recommends a contract type. Which contract type BEST protects the buyer from overpaying for uncertain scope?", listOf("A. Firm Fixed Price — locks in the price upfront", "B. Cost Plus Fixed Fee — seller is reimbursed actual costs plus a fixed fee", "C. Fixed Price Incentive Fee — incentivises seller performance", "D. Time and Material — pays for actual time and materials used"), 1, "With UNCERTAIN scope, a Cost Plus Fixed Fee (CPFF) contract is most appropriate. With FFP, the seller adds large contingency buffers for uncertainty (making it expensive) and may cut corners to stay within price. CPFF pays the seller's actual costs + a fixed fee — the buyer pays actual costs but gets transparent, quality work without the seller gaming the scope uncertainty. T&M is also reasonable but less structured."),
        QuizQuestion("q82", "procurement", "In an FPIF contract: Target Cost = \$200K, Target Price = \$220K, Ceiling Price = \$260K, Buyer Share Ratio = 80%. What is the Point of Total Assumption (PTA)?", listOf("A. \$225,000", "B. \$250,000", "C. \$245,000", "D. \$240,000"), 1, "PTA = ((Ceiling Price − Target Price) / Buyer Share Ratio) + Target Cost = ((260,000 − 220,000) / 0.80) + 200,000 = (40,000 / 0.80) + 200,000 = 50,000 + 200,000 = \$250,000. Above \$250K actual cost, the seller bears ALL overruns — the contract behaves like a fixed-price contract at that point."),
        QuizQuestion("q83", "procurement", "A vendor claims the project team caused scope changes that increased their costs, leading to a dispute. What should the PM do FIRST?", listOf("A. Immediately terminate the contract for non-performance", "B. Pay the vendor's additional costs to maintain the relationship", "C. Review the contract and procurement documentation to understand the terms and facts", "D. Escalate to senior management for a decision"), 2, "A.R.T.: First, ASSESS the situation — review the procurement contract, SOW, change logs, and communications to understand what was actually agreed and what changes occurred. This factual foundation is needed before any action. If a genuine contractual dispute exists after review, escalate to Claims Administration (not senior management). Termination is a last resort. Paying without review sets a bad precedent."),
        QuizQuestion("q84", "procurement", "During a bidder conference, one seller privately asks the PM a question about a technical requirement. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Answer the question privately to be helpful", "B. Answer the question and share the Q&A with all bidders", "C. Refuse to answer any questions outside the formal RFP process", "D. Direct the seller to submit the question in writing only"), 1, "Fairness (PMI Code of Ethics) requires that ALL bidders receive the same information. The PM should answer the question but share BOTH the question and answer with all bidders (typically via addendum to the RFP). Answering privately gives one bidder an unfair advantage. Refusing outright is unhelpful. Written-only is acceptable but the key is sharing with everyone."),
        QuizQuestion("q85", "procurement", "A buyer verbally instructs the seller to add a feature not in the contract. The seller complies. Later, the buyer refuses to pay for the extra work. What is this situation called?", listOf("A. Scope creep", "B. Constructive change", "C. Contract breach", "D. Gold plating"), 1, "A CONSTRUCTIVE CHANGE occurs when the buyer directs (even informally) work outside the contract scope without a formal change order. The seller performed the work in good faith and has a legitimate claim for compensation. This is why ALL contract changes must go through the formal contract change control process — to protect both parties."),
        QuizQuestion("q86", "procurement", "Which of the following is NOT typically one of the essential elements of a legally binding contract?", listOf("A. Offer and acceptance", "B. Consideration", "C. Detailed WBS", "D. Legal capacity of both parties"), 2, "The essential elements of a contract are: offer, acceptance, consideration, legal capacity, and legal purpose. A detailed WBS is a project management artifact — not a legal requirement for contract validity. Contracts may reference a statement of work, but a WBS is not a legal prerequisite."),
        QuizQuestion("q87", "procurement", "An agile project needs to procure software development services. Requirements will evolve sprint by sprint. Which contracting approach is MOST appropriate?", listOf("A. Fixed price for the entire project with detailed specifications", "B. Master Services Agreement with per-sprint statements of work and T&M pricing", "C. Cost plus fixed fee with a detailed 12-month plan", "D. Fixed price with penalty clauses for any scope changes"), 1, "Agile procurement uses a Master Services Agreement (MSA) establishing terms, with lighter-weight statements of work per sprint or release. T&M (or capped T&M) pricing accommodates evolving scope. Fixed price contracts penalise change — contrary to agile values. A detailed 12-month plan contradicts adaptive planning. Penalty clauses for scope changes are incompatible with \"responding to change.\""),
        QuizQuestion("q88", "procurement", "A seller and buyer disagree about whether a delivered component meets the contract specifications. The seller demands payment; the buyer refuses. What is the FIRST step?", listOf("A. Terminate the contract immediately", "B. File a lawsuit for breach of contract", "C. Attempt negotiation and resolution through the Claims Administration process", "D. Escalate to the project sponsor to make a decision"), 2, "Claims (disagreements about contract terms or compensation) should be resolved through Claims Administration — starting with NEGOTIATION between the parties. If negotiation fails: mediation → arbitration → litigation (as a last resort). Termination and lawsuits are extreme first steps. The sponsor does not have contractual authority to resolve a claim — that follows the contract's dispute resolution process."),
        QuizQuestion("q89", "leadership", "A project team is in the Storming stage. Two team members are in conflict over technical approach, and the tension is affecting team productivity. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Let the team resolve it themselves — this is normal for the Storming stage", "B. Force a decision by choosing the technical approach that makes most sense to you", "C. Meet with each team member individually, then bring them together for collaborative problem-solving", "D. Restructure the team to separate the conflicting members"), 2, "While Storming is normal, the PM should not passively wait when productivity is impacted. The correct approach: meet individually with each party first (understand each perspective without the other present), then bring them together to collaboratively solve the conflict. This is PMI's preferred conflict resolution approach (Collaborate/Problem-Solve). Forcing a decision damages team relationships. Restructuring is extreme and rarely necessary."),
        QuizQuestion("q90", "leadership", "According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, which of the following would MOTIVATE a team member rather than just prevent dissatisfaction?", listOf("A. A 10% salary increase", "B. Better office working conditions", "C. Formal recognition and a \"Stretch\" project with greater responsibility", "D. Assurance of job security for the next 2 years"), 2, "Herzberg's Motivators include: achievement, recognition, meaningful work, responsibility, advancement, and growth. Formal recognition + a challenging project with greater responsibility are MOTIVATORS — they create genuine job satisfaction. Salary, working conditions, and job security are Hygiene Factors — improving them prevents dissatisfaction but does NOT create motivation. This is one of Herzberg's key insights."),
        QuizQuestion("q91", "leadership", "A PM is new to a team of highly experienced senior engineers who need minimal direction. Which leadership style is MOST appropriate?", listOf("A. Autocratic — the PM must establish authority first", "B. Transactional — reward high performance and address underperformance", "C. Laissez-faire or delegating — provide autonomy to the expert team", "D. Democratic — involve everyone in all decisions"), 2, "For a highly experienced, expert team that is capable and motivated, a Laissez-faire/Delegating style is most appropriate — give the team autonomy to make decisions and self-organise. Autocratic or transactional styles would undermine their expertise and motivation. Democratic is good for decisions but may be too process-heavy for routine work of an expert team. Situational leadership: match style to team maturity."),
        QuizQuestion("q92", "leadership", "A newly hired developer is enthusiastic but has never used the project's technology stack. According to Situational Leadership, which style should the PM use?", listOf("A. S4 Delegating — give them autonomy to learn", "B. S1 Directing — provide specific instructions and close supervision", "C. S3 Supporting — share decision-making", "D. S2 Coaching — explain and encourage"), 1, "An enthusiastic beginner (high commitment, low competence) needs S1 — DIRECTING. They are motivated but do not yet have the skills to work independently. The leader provides clear instructions, close supervision, and structured guidance. As competence grows, the leader shifts to S2, S3, then S4."),
        QuizQuestion("q93", "leadership", "A team frequently argues about whether decisions should be made by the PM alone or by the whole team. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Assert authority and make all decisions to end the arguments", "B. Facilitate the team to create or update a Team Charter defining decision-making criteria", "C. Escalate to the sponsor to define the decision-making process", "D. Let the team vote each time a decision is needed"), 1, "This is a team norms issue — the Team Charter should define how decisions are made (which decisions are PM's, which are team consensus, which are PO's). The team creates the charter together, which builds buy-in. Asserting authority violates servant leadership. Escalating abdicates PM responsibility. Voting each time is inefficient and does not resolve the underlying ambiguity."),
        QuizQuestion("q94", "leadership", "The PM wants to reward a team member who worked 60-hour weeks to meet a deadline. What is the PMI concern with this approach?", listOf("A. No concern — rewarding hard work is always appropriate", "B. It may encourage an unsustainable overtime culture and violates sustainable pace principles", "C. The reward should go to the whole team, not one person", "D. The reward must be monetary to be effective"), 1, "Rewarding overtime sends the message that working excessive hours is valued — encouraging burnout and an unsustainable pace. PMI's resource management guidance says reward DESIRABLE behaviours. The better approach: address WHY 60-hour weeks were needed (poor planning? overcommitment?) and reward efficiency, quality, and collaboration instead. Sustainable pace is an agile principle too."),
        QuizQuestion("q95", "leadership", "Which statement correctly distinguishes coaching from mentoring?", listOf("A. Coaching is long-term; mentoring is short-term", "B. Coaching focuses on performance through questions; mentoring shares experience for career development", "C. Coaching is done by managers; mentoring by peers", "D. Coaching is for junior staff; mentoring is for senior staff"), 1, "COACHING: goal-focused performance improvement; the coach asks questions to help the person find their own answers; typically medium-term. MENTORING: long-term career development; the mentor shares their own experience and wisdom; relationship-based. Both can be done by anyone with the relevant experience — not tied to management levels or seniority."),
        QuizQuestion("q96", "leadership", "An agile team uses \"Fist of Five\" to check agreement on a proposed approach. Three members show 4 fingers, one shows 2 fingers. What should happen?", listOf("A. Proceed — the majority supports it", "B. Discuss the concerns of the member showing 2 fingers before proceeding", "C. Vote again until everyone shows 4 or 5", "D. The PM decides since consensus was not reached"), 1, "Fist of Five is a CONSENSUS technique. A 2 (or lower) typically means \"I have significant concerns.\" Consensus means everyone can live with the decision — a 2 signals they cannot yet. The correct step is to hear and address the concerns of the person showing 2, then re-check. Proceeding on majority ignores consensus. Repeated voting without discussion is coercive. PM deciding violates the team's autonomy."),
        QuizQuestion("q97", "people-advanced", "A senior developer publicly ridicules a junior developer's question in a team meeting. The junior goes quiet and does not ask questions again. Which psychological safety level has been damaged, and what should the PM do?", listOf("A. Inclusion safety — the PM should include the junior in more meetings", "B. Learner safety — the PM should address the senior's behaviour privately and re-establish that questions are welcome", "C. Contributor safety — the PM should assign the junior more tasks", "D. Challenger safety — the PM should encourage the junior to challenge the senior back"), 1, "Ridiculing a question attacks LEARNER safety — the belief that one can ask questions and make mistakes without humiliation. The PM must address the senior developer's behaviour (privately — modelling respect) and publicly re-establish that questions are welcome (e.g., asking questions themselves, thanking people who ask). Leaving it unaddressed signals the behaviour is acceptable and safety erodes for the whole team."),
        QuizQuestion("q98", "people-advanced", "A fire alarm sounds during a heated design debate. Two team members insist on finishing the argument before evacuating. Which TKI conflict mode should the PM use, and why?", listOf("A. Collaborating — find a solution that satisfies both", "B. Compromising — agree to finish in five minutes", "C. Competing — direct them to evacuate immediately", "D. Avoiding — let them finish since it is important to them"), 2, "Safety emergency = COMPETING (forcing) is correct. The PM directs immediate evacuation with no negotiation. Collaborating is PMI's general preference, but the exam tests situational judgment — in emergencies, unilateral direction is appropriate. This is why TKI has five modes: each fits a context."),
        QuizQuestion("q99", "people-advanced", "According to research on project conflict, which is the MOST common source of conflict on projects?", listOf("A. Personality clashes", "B. Technical disagreements", "C. Schedules", "D. Cost"), 2, "Schedules are the #1 source of project conflict, followed by priorities and resources. Personality clashes are the LEAST common — despite being the most talked about. This matters for diagnosis: when a \"personality conflict\" appears, dig for the underlying schedule or priority dispute driving it."),
        QuizQuestion("q100", "people-advanced", "Using non-violent communication, which feedback statement is correctly structured?", listOf("A. \"You are always late with your reports and it is unprofessional.\"", "B. \"When the report arrived two days after the deadline, I felt worried because the steering committee had to decide without it. Could you flag delays as soon as you see them?\"", "C. \"The report was late. Do better.\"", "D. \"Everyone else delivers on time — why can't you?\""), 1, "NVC structure: Observation (report arrived two days late) → Feeling (worried) → Need (committee needed it) → Request (flag delays early). It is specific, non-judgmental, and actionable. \"Always late\" and \"unprofessional\" are judgments; \"do better\" is vague; comparing to others shames. NVC addresses behaviour without attacking the person — preserving psychological safety."),
        QuizQuestion("q101", "people-advanced", "A PM in a weak-matrix organisation needs a functional manager to release a key resource for two weeks. The functional manager has refused twice. Which influence approach is MOST likely to succeed?", listOf("A. Escalate to the sponsor to force the release", "B. Frame the request in terms of the functional manager's own goals and offer reciprocal support", "C. Send a formal memo citing the project charter's authority", "D. Ask the resource to work on the project unofficially"), 1, "Influencing without authority: understand what the functional manager needs (their goals, constraints) and frame the request accordingly — perhaps the project outcome helps their department, or you can offer something in return (reciprocity). Escalation may work but damages the relationship and is a last resort. A memo citing authority is Legitimate power — weak in a matrix. Unofficial work is unethical."),
        QuizQuestion("q102", "people-advanced", "A team retrospective is consistently silent — the same two people speak and everyone else says \"no issues.\" What is the MOST likely cause and best response?", listOf("A. The team has no issues — shorten the retrospective", "B. Low psychological safety — use anonymous input methods and the PM should model vulnerability by sharing their own mistakes", "C. The team is disengaged — mandate participation", "D. The retrospective format is boring — try a new game"), 1, "Silence in retrospectives is a classic psychological safety symptom — people do not believe it is safe to raise problems. Format changes alone will not fix this. The PM should: (1) use anonymous input (sticky notes, digital tools) to lower the risk of speaking; (2) model vulnerability by sharing their own mistakes first; (3) respond visibly well when someone does raise an issue. Mandating participation increases fear."),
        QuizQuestion("q103", "people-advanced", "Two team members disagree on database design. Both approaches would work. The decision is needed by tomorrow. The PM has moderate technical knowledge. Which TKI mode fits BEST?", listOf("A. Competing — PM picks one to end the debate", "B. Collaborating — facilitate a working session to merge the best of both approaches", "C. Avoiding — let them keep debating", "D. Accommodating — go with the more senior person's preference"), 1, "Both approaches are viable, both parties are competent, and there is (just) enough time — this is a COLLABORATING situation. A facilitated session (timeboxed to fit the deadline) to identify decision criteria and possibly merge approaches produces a better outcome AND preserves both relationships. Competing risks a suboptimal choice by a less-expert PM. Accommodating on seniority alone ignores merit."),
        QuizQuestion("q104", "ethics", "Your sponsor instructs you to report the project status as \"on track\" to the steering committee, though you know the schedule has slipped by 3 weeks. She says the delay will be recovered before it matters. What should you do?", listOf("A. Comply — the sponsor has authority and the delay may be recovered", "B. Report the accurate status; offer to include the sponsor's recovery plan alongside it", "C. Report \"on track\" but privately inform one committee member of the truth", "D. Resign from the project to avoid the ethical dilemma"), 1, "Honesty (mandatory standard): do not engage in deceptive behaviour, half-truths, or withholding information. The status IS 3 weeks behind — that is a fact the steering committee needs for decision-making. The professional approach: report accurately AND present the recovery plan so the committee sees both the problem and the proposed solution. Private back-channel disclosure is still deception. Resigning avoids the responsibility to act ethically."),
        QuizQuestion("q105", "ethics", "You are leading vendor selection. You realise one bidder is a company where your spouse works as a senior manager. Your spouse is not involved in the bid. What should you do?", listOf("A. Continue — your spouse is not involved so there is no conflict", "B. Disclose the relationship immediately and recuse yourself from the evaluation", "C. Continue but be extra careful to be objective", "D. Ask your spouse to resign from the company"), 1, "This is a clear conflict of interest — a close personal relationship with a bidder. Fairness (mandatory): proactively and fully disclose any real or POTENTIAL conflict, and refrain from participating until stakeholders consent. Whether your spouse is directly involved is irrelevant — the PERCEIVED conflict undermines confidence in the process. Disclose in writing and recuse."),
        QuizQuestion("q106", "ethics", "In a country where your project operates, it is customary to pay a \"processing fee\" to a government clerk to expedite permits. Without it, permits take 6 months instead of 2 weeks. Your schedule depends on the 2-week timeline. What should you do?", listOf("A. Pay the fee — it is customary and the schedule depends on it", "B. Refuse to pay; assess the schedule impact; escalate to the sponsor; explore legal alternatives", "C. Pay the fee but record it as a legitimate expense", "D. Ask a local contractor to pay it on your behalf"), 1, "A facilitation payment to a government official to expedite a service is a bribe — prohibited by the PMI Code (Fairness, Honesty) and illegal under laws like the US FCPA and UK Bribery Act regardless of local custom. Using a contractor as an intermediary is still bribery. The correct path: refuse, assess the schedule impact, escalate, and explore legitimate options (expedited legal processes, schedule re-baseline). Ethics takes priority over schedule."),
        QuizQuestion("q107", "ethics", "Using PMI's Ethical Decision-Making Framework, what is the FIRST step when facing an ethical dilemma?", listOf("A. Identify all alternatives including doing nothing", "B. Apply ethical principles to the candidate decision", "C. Ensure you have all the facts — assessment", "D. Make the decision and take responsibility"), 2, "The EDMF starts with ASSESSMENT — ensuring you have all the facts. Just like A.R.T. for project decisions, ethical decisions begin with understanding the situation fully: What actually happened? Is it legal? Who is affected? Does it violate the Code? Only after assessment do you consider alternatives, analyse, apply principles, and act."),
        QuizQuestion("q108", "ethics", "A junior team member tells you in confidence that a senior team member has been sexually harassing her. She asks you not to do anything because she fears retaliation. What should you do?", listOf("A. Respect her wishes and do nothing — she asked for confidentiality", "B. Confront the senior team member privately", "C. Report the harassment through appropriate HR/management channels while protecting her from retaliation", "D. Move her to a different team quietly"), 2, "Respect (mandatory): do not act in an abusive manner; Responsibility (mandatory): report unethical conduct. Harassment is a mandatory-standard violation that MUST be reported — you cannot honour a request to conceal it. The PM should explain this to her compassionately, report through proper channels, and ensure protection from retaliation (also a mandatory standard). Quietly moving her punishes the victim and lets the behaviour continue."),
        QuizQuestion("q109", "ethics", "You used an AI tool to generate a risk assessment for your project. The output looks thorough. What is your ethical obligation before presenting it to stakeholders?", listOf("A. None — AI outputs are objective and can be presented as-is", "B. Review and validate the output with your own judgment, and disclose that AI assisted in creating it", "C. Present it as your own work since you prompted the AI", "D. Only use it internally — never present AI-generated content to stakeholders"), 1, "Honesty requires transparency about AI use; Responsibility requires you to remain accountable for the content. The PM must validate AI outputs (AI can hallucinate, encode bias, or miss context) and disclose AI assistance. Presenting it as purely your own work is deceptive. Banning AI use entirely is unnecessary — the 2026 ECO explicitly expects PMs to use AI responsibly."),
        QuizQuestion("q110", "business", "A PM is assigned to a project in a functional organisation. They need additional resources from the engineering department. Who must they negotiate with?", listOf("A. The project sponsor", "B. The PMO director", "C. The Functional Manager of the engineering department", "D. The portfolio manager"), 2, "In a functional organisation, the Functional Manager controls all resources within their department. PMs in functional organisations have little or no authority over resources outside their direct team. Any resource requests must be negotiated with and approved by the Functional Manager — this is one of the key challenges (and exam traps) of working in functional or weak-matrix structures."),
        QuizQuestion("q111", "business", "A project manager discovers the company has existing templates and a lessons learned repository from similar past projects. What type of asset is this?", listOf("A. Enterprise Environmental Factor (EEF) — external constraint", "B. Organisational Process Asset (OPA) — organisational knowledge the team can use", "C. Project management plan component", "D. Work performance information"), 1, "Templates and lessons learned repositories are Organisational Process Assets (OPA) — they are organisational knowledge specific to the company that the project team can use and update. EEF are conditions the team cannot control (regulations, market conditions, culture). OPA are assets the team actively leverages to improve project performance."),
        QuizQuestion("q112", "business", "Which document defines the processes for creating, tracking, and sustaining project benefits AFTER the project closes?", listOf("A. Project Charter", "B. Business Case", "C. Project Management Plan", "D. Benefits Management Plan"), 3, "The Benefits Management Plan documents how and when benefits will be delivered, the mechanisms to measure them, who is accountable for benefits realisation, and how benefits will be sustained after project closure. It differs from the Business Case (which justifies the project investment) and the Project Charter (which authorises it). The PM works with the Sponsor to keep all three aligned throughout the project."),
        QuizQuestion("q113", "business", "A new environmental regulation is announced that could significantly impact your project's manufacturing approach. What should the PM do FIRST?", listOf("A. Halt all project work until the regulation is fully understood", "B. Immediately present a change request to the CCB", "C. Assess the regulation's impact on the project and update the risk register", "D. Ask the project sponsor to negotiate an exemption from the regulation"), 2, "A.R.T.: First ASSESS — understand the regulation and its specific impact on scope, schedule, cost, and quality. Then update the Risk Register with any identified risks or opportunities. Only after thorough assessment should you proceed to review the plan and take action (raise change request if needed). Halting all work is extreme without analysis. Seeking exemption is unlikely and premature."),
        QuizQuestion("q114", "business", "Your organisation is adopting an AI tool to assist with project risk analysis. As PM, what is your PRIMARY responsibility regarding this tool?", listOf("A. Delegate all risk identification to the AI tool since it processes data faster", "B. Evaluate AI outputs critically, validate with expert judgment, and ensure responsible use aligned with organisational policy", "C. Reject the AI tool until it is proven 100% accurate", "D. Use the AI tool only for quantitative risk analysis — not qualitative"), 1, "PMI's position on AI: it augments the PM but never replaces PM judgment. The PM must evaluate AI outputs critically, validate them with expert judgment, document AI usage, and ensure use is ethical, responsible, and aligned with organisational AI policy. Delegating ALL risk identification to AI abdicates PM responsibility. Rejecting AI entirely ignores its value. There is no restriction to only quantitative analysis."),
        QuizQuestion("q115", "business", "Midway through project execution, stakeholder analysis reveals that a key department is highly resistant to the organisational change the project will deliver. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Escalate the resistance to the sponsor and ask them to mandate adoption", "B. Remove the resistant department from the project's stakeholder list", "C. Assess the root cause of resistance, engage the department proactively, and update the change management approach", "D. Proceed with the project as planned — resistance is expected and will resolve itself"), 2, "Organisational Change Management (a 2026 ECO Business Environment task): resistance must be understood and addressed proactively. Assess root cause first (workload concerns? Fear of job loss? Unclear benefits?), engage the department in dialogue, and update the change management approach to address their specific concerns. Escalating immediately bypasses the PM's responsibility to manage stakeholders. Removing them or ignoring resistance will create bigger problems at implementation."),
        QuizQuestion("q116", "business", "A project has been running for 6 months. The original business case assumed a 20% revenue increase, but market conditions have shifted significantly. The expected benefit is now only 8%. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Continue the project — stopping midway would waste sunk costs", "B. Immediately stop the project since benefits have dropped", "C. Reassess the business case and benefits management plan, and present findings to the sponsor for a go/no-go decision", "D. Update the risk register with the market change and continue"), 2, "Benefits realisation is a continuous responsibility (2026 ECO Business Environment). When circumstances change the projected value significantly, the PM must reassess the business case and benefits management plan, quantify the revised benefit, and present findings to the sponsor — who has authority to make the go/no-go decision. Sunk cost fallacy (option A) is not a valid reason to continue a project that no longer justifies investment. Simply updating the risk register (option D) without escalating the business case change is insufficient."),
        QuizQuestion("q117", "business", "A stakeholder is aware of an upcoming system change and understands how to use the new system, but actively resists adopting it. Using ADKAR, where is the barrier?", listOf("A. Awareness — they need more information", "B. Desire — they do not want the change", "C. Knowledge — they need more training", "D. Ability — they cannot perform the new process"), 1, "ADKAR: Awareness (they know) ✓. Knowledge (they understand how) ✓. But they RESIST — the barrier is DESIRE. More training (Knowledge) will not help — they already know how. The PM must address WHY they resist: what do they fear losing? What is in it for them? Desire barriers require engagement, addressing concerns, and demonstrating personal benefit — not more information or training."),
        QuizQuestion("q118", "business", "A senior engineer with 20 years of experience is retiring. Their knowledge of the legacy system is undocumented and critical. What type of knowledge is this, and how should it be transferred?", listOf("A. Explicit — write a manual before they leave", "B. Tacit — use shadowing, mentoring, and paired work to transfer experience", "C. Explicit — record video tutorials", "D. Tacit — it cannot be transferred; hire a replacement"), 1, "Experience-based knowledge that is hard to articulate is TACIT knowledge. It transfers through relationship-based methods: shadowing, pairing, mentoring, communities of practice, and storytelling — not through documentation alone. While some can be documented (making it explicit), the judgment and intuition require hands-on transfer. Planning this transfer BEFORE departure is a Business Environment (knowledge management) responsibility."),
        QuizQuestion("q119", "business", "A new data protection regulation comes into effect in 6 months affecting your project. The compliance requirement will add \$50K to the budget. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Ignore it — it is 6 months away and may change", "B. Add the compliance work as a risk in the risk register and monitor", "C. Assess impact, raise a change request for the additional scope/budget, and update the compliance plan", "D. Ask the sponsor to seek an exemption from the regulation"), 2, "Legal/regulatory compliance is MANDATORY — there is no option to ignore, defer, or seek exemption. The regulation WILL apply, so it is a certainty (issue/constraint), not a risk. The PM must: assess impact → raise a change request for the additional scope and \$50K → update the compliance management plan → ensure the project achieves compliance by the deadline. Six months is enough time IF action starts now."),
        QuizQuestion("q120", "business", "Which document should a PM reference to verify that a project is still aligned with organisational strategy?", listOf("A. The Project Charter", "B. The Business Case", "C. The Project Management Plan", "D. The Stakeholder Register"), 1, "The Business Case documents WHY the project exists — the business need, strategic alignment, expected benefits, and financial justification. It is the reference for whether the project still makes sense strategically. If the business case becomes invalid (market changes, strategy shifts), the project should be re-evaluated. The Charter authorises the project; the PM Plan describes how; neither validates ongoing strategic alignment."),
        QuizQuestion("q121", "business", "In Kotter's 8-Step Change Model, why are \"short-term wins\" important?", listOf("A. They reduce the total cost of change", "B. They build momentum, credibility, and demonstrate the change is working", "C. They allow the change to be completed faster", "D. They replace the need for a compelling vision"), 1, "Kotter's Step 6 — Generate Short-Term Wins — creates visible evidence that the change is working. This builds credibility for the change initiative, converts skeptics, rewards early adopters, and maintains momentum. Without short-term wins, people lose faith and resistance grows. Short-term wins complement (not replace) the vision from Step 3."),
        QuizQuestion("q122", "benefits", "A project delivered a new CRM system on time, on budget, with all features. Six months later, only 20% of sales staff use it; the rest still use spreadsheets. According to PMBOK 8 value principles, how should this be assessed?", listOf("A. Successful — all triple-constraint targets met", "B. Unsuccessful — outputs delivered but outcomes and benefits not realised", "C. Partially successful — 20% adoption is a start", "D. Success cannot be judged until 12 months have passed"), 1, "PMBOK 8 and the 2026 ECO define success by VALUE delivered, not outputs produced. A CRM with 20% adoption has not achieved its outcome (changed sales behaviour) or benefits (pipeline visibility, efficiency). The output was delivered; the value was not. This is why adoption rate is a critical outcome metric and why change management is now a Business Environment task."),
        QuizQuestion("q123", "benefits", "A value stream map shows a feature takes 60 days from request to production, with 9 days of actual development and testing work. What is the Process Cycle Efficiency, and what does it indicate?", listOf("A. 15% — typical; most time is waiting and handoffs", "B. 85% — highly efficient process", "C. 6.7% — the team is slow", "D. 60% — acceptable"), 0, "PCE = VA Time ÷ Lead Time = 9 ÷ 60 = 15%. This means 85% of the lead time (51 days) is non-value-added — waiting for approvals, queues, handoffs. This is typical for unoptimised processes. The improvement opportunity is NOT to make developers faster (they only account for 9 days) but to eliminate the 51 days of waste — the classic Lean insight."),
        QuizQuestion("q124", "benefits", "Which of the following is a correctly written Key Result?", listOf("A. \"Launch the new mobile app by Q3\"", "B. \"Improve customer satisfaction\"", "C. \"Increase mobile app 30-day retention from 22% to 40% by end of Q3\"", "D. \"Complete all planned sprints\""), 2, "A Key Result must be MEASURABLE (a number), OUTCOME-focused (not an activity), and TIME-BOUND. \"Increase retention from 22% to 40% by Q3\" has a baseline, target, and deadline, and measures an outcome. \"Launch the app\" and \"complete sprints\" are activities/outputs, not results. \"Improve satisfaction\" has no metric."),
        QuizQuestion("q125", "benefits", "After project closure, who is accountable for measuring and reporting whether the projected \$2M annual savings were realised?", listOf("A. The project manager", "B. The benefits owner in the operations organisation", "C. The PMO", "D. The project team"), 1, "Benefits typically materialise AFTER the project closes — often over years. The PM and team have moved on. Accountability sits with the BENEFITS OWNER — a business-side role (usually in operations) named in the Benefits Management Plan. The PM's responsibility is to ensure tracking mechanisms and ownership are in place at handover. The PMO may aggregate and audit but does not own individual benefits."),
        QuizQuestion("q126", "benefits", "Two features are competing for the next sprint. Feature A: Cost of Delay \$40K/week, size 8 points. Feature B: Cost of Delay \$30K/week, size 3 points. Using WSJF, which goes first?", listOf("A. Feature A — higher cost of delay", "B. Feature B — WSJF of 10 vs 5", "C. Feature A — WSJF of 5 vs 10", "D. Equal — need more information"), 1, "WSJF = Cost of Delay ÷ Job Size. Feature A: 40 ÷ 8 = 5. Feature B: 30 ÷ 3 = 10. Feature B has the higher WSJF and goes first. The logic: B delivers most of its value quickly (small job), then A can start. If you did A first, you would forgo \$30K/week on B for 8 points of effort. WSJF favours small, valuable jobs."),
        QuizQuestion("q127", "benefits", "A PM wants to know during the project whether the new system will achieve its target benefit of 50% faster processing. Which is the BEST leading indicator to track?", listOf("A. Annual cost savings", "B. Pilot-group processing time on the beta release", "C. Post-launch customer satisfaction survey", "D. Year-end ROI calculation"), 1, "A LEADING indicator predicts future value while there is still time to act. Pilot-group processing time on a beta release directly measures whether the 50% target is achievable — during the project. Annual savings, post-launch surveys, and year-end ROI are LAGGING indicators — they confirm value only after it is too late to change course."),
        QuizQuestion("q128", "ai", "An AI scheduling tool forecasts project completion on 20 October with 85% confidence. The team's bottom-up estimate says 5 December. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Commit to 20 October — the AI has more data", "B. Commit to 5 December — human estimates always override AI", "C. Investigate the divergence: examine assumptions on both sides before committing to any date", "D. Commit to the midpoint — 12 November"), 2, "A large divergence between AI forecast and team estimate is a signal to INVESTIGATE, not to pick a side or average. Perhaps the AI lacks context on a new technology; perhaps the team is padding estimates. Understanding WHY they differ produces a better estimate than either alone. PMI's model: AI augments judgment — it does not replace it, nor is it ignored."),
        QuizQuestion("q129", "ai", "A PM uses a public AI chatbot to draft a stakeholder communication, pasting in the full stakeholder register including personal contact details and engagement assessments. What is the primary concern?", listOf("A. The AI may write a poor-quality email", "B. Confidential and personal data has been shared with an external system without authorisation", "C. The stakeholders may object to AI-drafted communication", "D. Prompt engineering was not applied correctly"), 1, "Pasting personal data and confidential assessments into a public AI tool is a data protection and confidentiality breach — violating the Respect value (property rights), likely data protection law (GDPR etc.), and organisational policy. This is the #1 practical AI risk on projects. The PM should have anonymised the data or used an approved internal tool."),
        QuizQuestion("q130", "ai", "An AI resource-allocation tool consistently recommends the same three senior engineers for high-visibility work, while junior and female engineers are recommended for maintenance tasks. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Follow the recommendations — the AI is optimising for success", "B. Investigate for algorithmic bias; ensure fair opportunity; make allocation decisions with human judgment", "C. Stop using the tool immediately and report the vendor", "D. Adjust the AI's parameters to force equal distribution"), 1, "The pattern suggests algorithmic bias — the AI is likely reproducing historical patterns (senior engineers got past opportunities, so it predicts they will succeed again). Fairness (PMI Code) requires equal access to opportunity. The PM should investigate, apply human judgment to allocation, and ensure development opportunities are distributed fairly. Blind compliance perpetuates bias; forced equal distribution ignores genuine capability differences."),
        QuizQuestion("q131", "ai", "A regulator audits a pharmaceutical project and asks which risk assessments were AI-generated. The PM did not track this. What ethical and governance failure does this represent?", listOf("A. None — AI use does not need to be tracked", "B. Failure of transparency and traceability — AI-assisted artifacts should be marked and documented", "C. Failure to use AI correctly", "D. The regulator has no authority over AI use"), 1, "Transparency (Honesty) and accountability (Responsibility) require that AI-assisted artifacts be identified and traceable — especially in regulated industries where audit trails are mandatory. The AI governance framework should specify how AI use is disclosed and documented. Without this, the PM cannot demonstrate that AI outputs were properly validated."),
        QuizQuestion("q132", "ai", "Which activity is LEAST appropriate to delegate to an AI tool?", listOf("A. Drafting the first version of a weekly status report", "B. Running Monte Carlo simulation on the schedule", "C. Mediating a conflict between two team members", "D. Extracting action items from a meeting transcript"), 2, "Conflict mediation requires emotional intelligence, trust, relationship, and nuanced human judgment — core People-domain skills where AI adds no value and may cause harm. Drafting reports, running simulations, and extracting action items are pattern-based tasks where AI excels (with human review). PMI's guidance: use AI for analysis and drafting; keep human relationships human."),
        QuizQuestion("q133", "ai", "The organisation has no AI usage policy. A team member proposes using an AI tool to generate test cases. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Allow it — no policy means no restriction", "B. Prohibit it — no policy means it is not permitted", "C. Assess the risks (data, IP, accuracy), define interim guardrails, and propose a policy to the PMO/governance body", "D. Wait until a policy is published before using any AI"), 2, "Absence of policy is not permission or prohibition — it is a governance gap the PM should address. The Business Environment task \"Manage and implement AI\" expects the PM to assess risks, establish appropriate guardrails for the project, and escalate the need for organisational policy. Blanket prohibition forgoes value; blanket permission ignores risk; waiting indefinitely stalls the project."),
        QuizQuestion("q134", "tailoring", "A project manager is about to start a project developing a new medical device. Requirements are clearly defined by regulatory authorities. What development approach is most appropriate?", listOf("A. Adaptive — allows flexibility as requirements evolve", "B. Predictive — requirements are well-defined and change is costly", "C. Hybrid — use agile for design and waterfall for regulatory submissions", "D. Scrum — allows frequent delivery and stakeholder feedback"), 1, "When requirements are clearly defined (by regulatory standards in this case), change is costly/regulated, and compliance is critical — a Predictive (waterfall) approach is most appropriate. The detailed schedule, formal change control, and documented baselines of predictive PM align with regulatory requirements. Agile requires flexibility in scope; regulatory environments often prohibit that."),
        QuizQuestion("q135", "tailoring", "A project team in an adaptive lifecycle is struggling with scope management — stakeholders keep requesting changes mid-sprint that disrupt the team. What is the BEST approach?", listOf("A. Stop accepting all new requests until the project ends", "B. Switch to a predictive lifecycle with formal change control", "C. Educate stakeholders about the backlog process — all changes go to the backlog for the next Sprint Planning", "D. Ask the sponsor to communicate that no changes are allowed"), 2, "In agile/Scrum, changes are embraced — but they go into the PRODUCT BACKLOG, not directly into the current sprint. The Product Owner manages and prioritises the backlog. New requests during a sprint should be captured in the backlog and prioritised for future sprints. Sprint Goals must be protected. Educating stakeholders on the process (add to backlog → prioritise → next sprint) is the correct agile response."),
        QuizQuestion("q136", "tailoring", "A PM is starting a 6-person, 3-month internal tool development project in a fast-moving startup. Which tailoring decision is MOST appropriate?", listOf("A. Full PMBOK processes with formal CCB and EVM reporting", "B. Lightweight: one-page charter, Kanban board, weekly demos, risk board", "C. No process at all — startups move fast", "D. Formal phase gates with steering committee reviews"), 1, "Small team, short duration, low criticality, innovative culture → lightweight tailoring. A one-page charter, Kanban for flow, weekly demos for stakeholder feedback, and a simple risk board provide adequate governance without overhead. \"No process\" is never correct — even startups need some governance. Full PMBOK or phase gates would be over-tailoring for this context."),
        QuizQuestion("q137", "tailoring", "A team adopts Scrum but decides to skip retrospectives \"because we don't have time.\" Six months later, the same problems keep recurring. What tailoring mistake was made?", listOf("A. Over-tailoring", "B. Cherry-picking — removing an element without understanding its purpose", "C. Under-tailoring", "D. Tailoring by regulation"), 1, "Retrospectives are Scrum's continuous improvement mechanism. Removing them without understanding their purpose is cherry-picking — taking the convenient parts of a method while dropping the parts that make it work. The recurring problems are the direct consequence: no mechanism to inspect and adapt the process. Tailoring must be justified by project characteristics, not by convenience."),
        QuizQuestion("q138", "tailoring", "A medical device project has both regulated hardware (FDA-controlled) and companion mobile app software. How should governance be tailored?", listOf("A. Apply FDA-level rigour to everything for consistency", "B. Apply lightweight agile governance to everything for speed", "C. Hybrid: formal phase gates and documentation for hardware; sprints with documentation-in-DoD for software; unified risk register", "D. Manage as two completely separate projects"), 2, "Hybrid tailoring applies the right governance to each component. Hardware under FDA regulation needs formal gates, traceability, and documentation. Software can use sprints with regulatory documentation built into the Definition of Done. A unified risk register and integrated schedule ensure the components stay aligned. Uniform heavy governance slows software unnecessarily; uniform light governance fails regulatory compliance; separate projects lose integration."),
        QuizQuestion("q139", "tailoring", "A PM documents that the project will skip quantitative risk analysis. A PMO auditor asks why. Which response demonstrates correct tailoring practice?", listOf("A. \"We didn't have time for it.\"", "B. \"The project is under \$200K with low complexity and a risk-tolerant sponsor; qualitative analysis is sufficient and documented in the PM plan.\"", "C. \"Our team doesn't know how to do Monte Carlo.\"", "D. \"Quantitative analysis is optional in PMBOK 8.\""), 1, "Correct tailoring is JUSTIFIED by project characteristics (size, complexity, risk threshold) and DOCUMENTED in the PM plan. Option B cites specific criteria and confirms documentation. \"No time\" and \"don't know how\" are convenience/capability excuses, not tailoring rationale. \"Optional in PMBOK\" is true but not a justification — the question is whether it is appropriate for THIS project."),
        QuizQuestion("q140", "tailoring", "Midway through a predictive project, requirements are changing every week and rework is mounting. What does this diagnostic signal suggest?", listOf("A. Increase change control rigour to stop the changes", "B. The development approach is mismatched to uncertainty — consider shifting toward adaptive/hybrid", "C. Extend the schedule to accommodate changes", "D. Replace the requirements analyst"), 1, "Requirements churn and rework in a predictive project signal that the approach does not fit the actual uncertainty level. The tailoring diagnostic response is to re-tailor: shift toward adaptive or hybrid for the volatile components, add prototyping, shorten feedback loops. Tightening change control fights the symptom, not the cause. Extending the schedule accepts the waste. Replacing people is never a first response."),
        QuizQuestion("q141", "agile", "During Sprint Planning, the team selects items from the Product Backlog and commits to a Sprint Goal. Who is responsible for creating the estimates for the selected backlog items?", listOf("A. The Product Owner, since they know the business value", "B. The Scrum Master, since they manage the process", "C. The Developers, since they will do the work", "D. The project sponsor, since they own the budget"), 2, "In Scrum, DEVELOPERS create their own estimates. Only the people doing the work can accurately estimate effort — they have the technical expertise (SME). The Product Owner provides priorities and business value; the Scrum Master facilitates. External estimation by management violates the self-organising principle and reduces estimate accuracy and team ownership."),
        QuizQuestion("q142", "agile", "A stakeholder requests a new high-priority feature halfway through a Sprint. What should happen?", listOf("A. Add it to the current Sprint Backlog immediately since it is high priority", "B. Cancel the Sprint and restart with the new feature included", "C. Add it to the Product Backlog and the PO will prioritise it for the next Sprint", "D. Implement it quietly after regular hours without changing the Sprint Backlog"), 2, "New requests go to the PRODUCT BACKLOG — not into the current sprint. The Sprint Goal and Sprint Backlog should not change during a Sprint (unless the Sprint Goal itself becomes obsolete). The Product Owner adds it to the backlog, prioritises it, and the team will consider it in the next Sprint Planning. Only the PO can cancel a Sprint, and only if the Sprint Goal becomes invalid."),
        QuizQuestion("q143", "agile", "A Scrum team's velocity over the last 5 sprints has been: 22, 18, 20, 24, 21 story points. The next sprint has 30 story points planned. What is the concern?", listOf("A. No concern — velocity varies and the team can stretch for one sprint", "B. The sprint is significantly overcommitted — average velocity is about 21 points", "C. The Product Owner should add more items to fill remaining capacity", "D. The Scrum Master should ask the team to work overtime"), 1, "Average velocity = (22+18+20+24+21)/5 = 105/5 = 21 story points per sprint. Planning 30 points is ~43% above the team's historical capacity — a significant overcommitment. Sustainable pace is a core agile principle. Overcommitting leads to burnout, incomplete work, quality issues, and eroded trust with stakeholders. The team should plan to approximately 21 points, not 30."),
        QuizQuestion("q144", "agile", "What is the PRIMARY difference between a Sprint Review and a Sprint Retrospective?", listOf("A. The Review is internal; the Retrospective includes stakeholders", "B. The Review improves the product; the Retrospective improves the team and process", "C. The Retrospective is held before the Review", "D. The Review is optional; the Retrospective is mandatory"), 1, "Sprint Review = inspects and adapts the PRODUCT (what was built) — includes stakeholders. Sprint Retrospective = inspects and adapts the TEAM and PROCESS (how it was built) — Scrum Team only, no external stakeholders. The Review comes BEFORE the Retrospective at the end of a sprint. Both are mandatory Scrum events."),
        QuizQuestion("q145", "agile", "A large organisation with 80 developers across 10 teams wants to coordinate their agile work with quarterly planning cycles. Which scaling framework is MOST likely appropriate?", listOf("A. Scrum of Scrums", "B. SAFe (Scaled Agile Framework)", "C. LeSS", "D. Nexus"), 1, "SAFe is designed for large enterprises with 50–125+ people organised into Agile Release Trains, using quarterly Program Increment (PI) Planning. 80 developers across 10 teams with quarterly cycles fits SAFe precisely. Scrum of Scrums is lightweight coordination for a few teams. LeSS handles 2–8 teams on one product. Nexus handles 3–9 teams — closer but SAFe's quarterly PI Planning matches the stated requirement."),
        QuizQuestion("q146", "agile", "A software team builds several features that were never requested by users, based on what developers thought would be \"cool.\" In Lean terms, this is which type of waste?", listOf("A. Defects", "B. Inventory", "C. Overproduction", "D. Over-processing"), 2, "Building things nobody asked for is OVERPRODUCTION — the classic Lean waste. It consumed effort, added complexity, and delivered no value. This is the same concept as \"gold plating\" in predictive PM. Overproduction is considered the worst waste because it causes other wastes (inventory of unused features, defects in unnecessary code, etc.)."),
        QuizQuestion("q147", "agile", "On a Cumulative Flow Diagram, the \"In Testing\" band has been widening steadily for three weeks. What does this indicate?", listOf("A. Testing capacity has increased", "B. More items are entering testing than leaving — a bottleneck in testing", "C. The team is producing fewer defects", "D. The sprint length should be extended"), 1, "On a CFD, each band represents items in a workflow state. A WIDENING band means items are entering that state faster than they are leaving — work is accumulating there. This indicates a BOTTLENECK in testing. The team should investigate (test capacity? environment issues? unclear acceptance criteria?) and address it — possibly by having developers help with testing."),
        QuizQuestion("q148", "agile", "A pharmaceutical company uses agile for drug discovery software but must produce regulatory documentation for FDA submission. What is the correct approach?", listOf("A. Abandon agile — regulated environments require predictive only", "B. Produce documentation incrementally alongside each increment; use hybrid governance", "C. Use agile for development and switch to predictive for documentation at the end", "D. Skip documentation until the product is complete, then document retroactively"), 1, "Agile CAN work in regulated environments — documentation is produced INCREMENTALLY as part of each sprint (added to Definition of Done), not skipped or deferred. This is a hybrid approach: agile development with regulatory documentation as a sprint deliverable. Retroactive documentation is risky and often incomplete. Switching to predictive at the end creates a \"big bang\" documentation phase — exactly what agile avoids."),
        QuizQuestion("q149", "agile", "Which statement about Disciplined Agile (DA) is correct?", listOf("A. DA is a prescriptive framework requiring specific ceremonies", "B. DA is PMI's toolkit approach that lets teams choose their way of working", "C. DA replaces Scrum and Kanban", "D. DA is only for large-scale enterprise projects"), 1, "Disciplined Agile (DA) is PMI's agile approach — a TOOLKIT (not a prescriptive framework) that helps teams \"choose their way of working\" (WoW). It incorporates Scrum, Kanban, XP, SAFe, and other practices, letting teams select what fits their context. It is hybrid-friendly and scalable from small teams to enterprises. It does not replace other frameworks — it draws from them."),
    )

    val mixedTwenty: List<QuizQuestion> = listOf(
        QuizQuestion("q1", "blueprint", "Your project is behind schedule. A stakeholder demands you accelerate delivery without increasing budget. What is your FIRST step?", listOf("A. Add more resources to the critical path immediately", "B. Reduce project scope to meet the deadline", "C. Communicate the implications and discuss alternatives with the stakeholder", "D. Cut corners to save time and meet the deadline"), 2, "PMI always favours communication and collaboration first. Assess the situation, communicate trade-offs and implications to the stakeholder, and collaboratively determine the best path forward. Crashing (adding resources) costs money — contradicting the \"no budget increase\" constraint. Scope reduction needs formal change control. Cutting corners violates quality standards and ethics."),
        QuizQuestion("q2", "blueprint", "A team member tells you the project budget might be exceeded next quarter if a vendor delivers late. What is this — and what do you do FIRST?", listOf("A. It is an issue — update the issue log and escalate immediately", "B. It is a risk — update the risk register and analyze it", "C. It is a constraint — update the project charter", "D. It is a problem — call an emergency team meeting now"), 1, "\"Might\" signals uncertainty about a future event — this is a RISK, not an issue. First action: document it in the Risk Register. Then perform qualitative risk analysis to prioritise it. Then plan a risk response. The Issue Log is for problems that have already occurred. Escalation is premature without analysis."),
        QuizQuestion("q3", "blueprint", "A senior executive asks you to bypass change control for a minor scope addition she personally approved. What do you do?", listOf("A. Implement the change since a senior executive approved it", "B. Submit a formal change request and follow the change control process", "C. Ask the team to implement it quietly after hours", "D. Tell the executive it is not possible under any circumstances"), 1, "ALL changes must go through Integrated Change Control regardless of who requests it or how minor it seems. The PM's job is to follow the process — not circumvent it under authority pressure. Submitting a formal change request protects the project, the PM, and ensures the impact is properly assessed and approved."),
        QuizQuestion("q4", "blueprint", "A candidate is preparing for the July 2026 PMP exam using a 2023 study guide. What is the MOST significant risk to their preparation?", listOf("A. The guide uses PMBOK 7 instead of PMBOK 8", "B. The guide under-invests in Business Environment by a factor of three", "C. The guide uses 230-minute timing instead of 240", "D. The guide covers 35 tasks instead of 26"), 1, "The most significant impact of the 2026 ECO is that Business Environment tripled from 8% to 26%. A 2023 guide would allocate minimal time to this domain, leaving the candidate unprepared for roughly a quarter of the exam. While the other changes are real, they are less impactful on actual exam performance."),
        QuizQuestion("q5", "blueprint", "During the exam, you encounter a network diagram question asking for the critical path. Activities: A(3)→B(4)→D(2) and A(3)→C(6)→D(2). What is the critical path duration?", listOf("A. 9 days", "B. 11 days", "C. 14 days", "D. 15 days"), 1, "Path 1: A→B→D = 3+4+2 = 9 days. Path 2: A→C→D = 3+6+2 = 11 days. The critical path is the LONGEST path = A→C→D = 11 days. Activities on this path (A, C, D) have zero float. Activity B has 2 days of float (11−9)."),
        QuizQuestion("q6", "blueprint", "You are 90 minutes into the exam and have answered 60 questions. You are on pace for how many questions in 240 minutes?", listOf("A. 120 questions — you will run out of time", "B. 160 questions — you will run out of time", "C. 180 questions — you are exactly on pace", "D. 200 questions — you have spare time"), 1, "60 questions in 90 min = 1.5 min per question. At that rate, 240 min ÷ 1.5 = 160 questions. You will NOT finish all 180 questions. You need to speed up to ~1.33 min/question. This is why time management practice is critical — many candidates know the content but fail on time."),
        QuizQuestion("q7", "blueprint", "On a Kanban board shown in a graphic question, the \"In Review\" column has 8 items but a WIP limit of 3. What does this indicate and what should the team do?", listOf("A. The team is highly productive — increase the WIP limit", "B. There is a bottleneck in review — the team should stop pulling new work and swarm on reviews", "C. The WIP limit was set too low — remove it", "D. The reviewers should be replaced with faster reviewers"), 1, "A column exceeding its WIP limit signals a bottleneck. In Kanban, the response is to STOP pulling new work into the system and focus the team on clearing the bottleneck (swarming). Raising the WIP limit hides the problem. Removing the limit defeats the purpose of Kanban. Replacing people is never a first response."),
        QuizQuestion("q8", "blueprint", "A case study describes a hybrid project where the infrastructure component uses predictive methods and the software component uses Scrum. A question asks how the PM should handle a change request to the infrastructure scope. What is correct?", listOf("A. Add it to the product backlog for the Product Owner to prioritise", "B. Submit a formal change request through Integrated Change Control", "C. Discuss it in the next sprint retrospective", "D. Implement it immediately since the project is agile"), 1, "In a hybrid project, the PREDICTIVE component follows predictive rules — formal change control. The infrastructure scope is predictive, so a formal change request through the CCB is correct. Only the software (Scrum) component would use backlog prioritisation. Hybrid means applying the right process to each component — not blending them randomly."),
        QuizQuestion("q9", "pmbok8", "In PMBOK 8, which performance domain absorbed both Stakeholder Management AND Communications Management from PMBOK 6?", listOf("A. Governance", "B. Resources", "C. Stakeholders", "D. Finance"), 2, "The Stakeholders performance domain in PMBOK 8 consolidates both Stakeholder Management and Communications Management from PMBOK 6. PMI recognised that communication and stakeholder engagement are inseparable — effective communication IS stakeholder engagement."),
        QuizQuestion("q10", "pmbok8", "A project team is midway through execution. A team member asks when monitoring activities should begin. What is the correct answer?", listOf("A. After execution is complete", "B. At the start of the closing focus area", "C. Monitoring runs in parallel with all focus areas throughout the entire project", "D. Monitoring begins only when problems arise"), 2, "Monitoring and Controlling is a Focus Area that runs IN PARALLEL with all other Focus Areas throughout the entire project — from initiation through closing. It is not sequential. Waiting until execution is complete or until problems arise would leave the project uncontrolled."),
        QuizQuestion("q11", "pmbok8", "Which PMBOK 8 principle explicitly addresses environmental, social, and economic impacts — considering the needs of community, society, and future generations?", listOf("A. Adopt a Holistic View", "B. Focus on Value", "C. Build an Empowered Culture", "D. Integrate Sustainability Within All Project Areas"), 3, "Principle 5 — Integrate Sustainability — specifically broadens the PM orientation to consider community, society, and posterity (future generations). It introduces the triple bottom line (people, profit, planet) and three maturity levels of PM orientation: compliance-oriented, achievement-oriented, and sustainability-oriented."),
        QuizQuestion("q12", "pmbok8", "Which PMBOK 8 performance domain is the ONLY one with processes in all five Focus Areas (Initiating through Closing)?", listOf("A. Scope", "B. Stakeholders", "C. Governance", "D. Risk"), 2, "Governance is the only domain with processes in all 5 Focus Areas: Initiate Project/Phase (Initiating), Integrate & Align Plans (Planning), Manage Execution (Executing), Monitor & Control Performance (M&C), and Close Project/Phase (Closing). It is the integrating domain — like Integration Management in PMBOK 6. Stakeholders has Initiating but not Closing. Scope, Schedule, Finance, Resources, Risk have no Initiating or Closing processes."),
        QuizQuestion("q13", "pmbok8", "A project delivers all scope on time and on budget, but the intended business benefits are never realised because market conditions changed. According to PMBOK 8, was this project successful?", listOf("A. Yes — it met all triple constraint targets", "B. No — PMBOK 8 defines success by outcomes and value, not just outputs", "C. Partially — schedule and cost were met, so it is 66% successful", "D. It depends on whether the sponsor was satisfied"), 1, "PMBOK 8 (and the Focus on Value principle) defines project success by whether it delivered VALUE and realised BENEFITS — not just whether outputs were delivered on time and on budget. A project that delivers all outputs but no value has technically failed by PMBOK 8 standards. This is a key mindset shift from PMBOK 6."),
        QuizQuestion("q14", "pmbok8", "Which PMBOK 8 term describes a negative consequence of project outputs that must be identified and managed alongside benefits?", listOf("A. Residual risk", "B. Disbenefit", "C. Negative float", "D. Adverse outcome"), 1, "A \"Disbenefit\" is a negative consequence of project outputs — a term formalised in PMBOK 8. For example, a new system may deliver efficiency benefits but create a disbenefit of staff reduction or training burden. Disbenefits must be identified in the Benefits Management Plan and managed just like benefits. Residual risk refers to remaining risk after responses; negative float relates to schedule."),
        QuizQuestion("q15", "pmbok8", "According to PMBOK 8, what is the difference between an \"outcome\" and an \"output\"?", listOf("A. They are synonyms — both mean deliverables", "B. Output is what is produced; outcome is the result/consequence/change it enables", "C. Outcome is produced by the team; output is produced by the vendor", "D. Output is tangible; outcome is always intangible"), 1, "An OUTPUT is what the project produces — the deliverable (e.g., a software application). An OUTCOME is the end result or change that the output enables (e.g., 30% reduction in processing time). PMBOK 8 emphasises that PMs must focus on outcomes and value, not just producing outputs. A perfect output that produces no desired outcome is a failed project."),
        QuizQuestion("q16", "pmbok8", "A PM is tailoring the governance approach for a small internal project in an agile organisation. According to PMBOK 8, what level of governance is appropriate?", listOf("A. Comprehensive governance — all projects need full governance", "B. Lightweight governance appropriate to adaptive methods and small scale", "C. No governance — agile projects are self-governing", "D. Moderate governance combining predictive controls with agile flexibility"), 1, "PMBOK 8 explicitly states governance should be tailored: lightweight for adaptive/agile methods and small projects; moderate for hybrid; comprehensive for large predictive portfolios. A small internal agile project needs proportionate, lightweight governance — not the full apparatus of a large regulated project. But \"no governance\" is never correct — every project needs some governance framework."),
        QuizQuestion("q17", "mindset", "Two team members have a technical conflict. You have spoken to each individually. What is your NEXT step?", listOf("A. Escalate to the project sponsor to make the final decision", "B. Force a decision by choosing the technical approach yourself", "C. Bring both team members together to collaboratively solve the issue", "D. Document the conflict in the issue log and move forward without resolution"), 2, "After meeting individually (correct first step), the next step is to bring both parties together for collaborative problem-solving — PMI's preferred conflict resolution technique. Escalation and forcing are last resorts. Documenting without resolution avoids the conflict entirely, which almost never resolves underlying issues."),
        QuizQuestion("q18", "mindset", "During a sprint retrospective, the team identifies that they consistently overcommit to sprint goals. What should the Scrum Master do?", listOf("A. Tell the team they need to work faster to meet commitments", "B. Facilitate a discussion on estimation accuracy and sustainable pace", "C. Ask the Product Owner to reduce the number of backlog items", "D. Report the performance issue to the project sponsor"), 1, "The Scrum Master facilitates retrospectives and coaches the team — this is a coaching/process improvement situation. Overcommitment is an estimation and sustainable pace issue. Facilitating a team discussion on improving their estimation approach (story points, planning poker) and calibrating velocity accurately addresses the root cause. Telling the team to work faster violates sustainable pace principles."),
        QuizQuestion("q19", "mindset", "A project manager discovers that a team member has been reporting task status as \"complete\" when work is only partially done. What should the PM do FIRST?", listOf("A. Escalate immediately to the team member's functional manager", "B. Meet with the team member privately to understand the situation and address it", "C. Remove the team member from the project for dishonesty", "D. Send an email to all stakeholders warning them that status reports may be inaccurate"), 1, "A.R.T.: First, meet privately with the team member to understand why this is happening (Assess). There may be pressure to appear on track, misunderstanding of \"done,\" or other root causes. Jumping to escalation, removal, or stakeholder announcements before understanding the situation is premature and could damage trust and morale unnecessarily."),
        QuizQuestion("q20", "mindset", "A team member from a different cultural background rarely speaks up in meetings, and other members interpret this as disengagement. What should the PM do?", listOf("A. Tell the team member they need to participate more actively", "B. Recognise this may be a cultural communication style; create alternative channels for input", "C. Reassign the team member to a role with less meeting involvement", "D. Ask the team to be more direct in asking the team member for opinions"), 1, "This is a cultural intelligence scenario. In many cultures, speaking up in group meetings — especially to disagree — is inappropriate. The PM should recognise this, avoid misinterpreting silence as disengagement, and create alternative channels (one-on-one conversations, written input, smaller groups) where the team member can contribute comfortably. Telling them to change or reassigning them is culturally insensitive and violates the Respect value."),
    )
}